('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'nicotine-s-addictive-properties-importance', '466', 'of nicotine''s addictive properties and the importance of nicotine to cigarette smokers.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', '83250863-0873-0863-0865-us-55673', '468', '83250863-0873 at 0863-0865 (US 55673).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'augmentation-project-h-j-minnemeyer', '468', 'Augmentation Project," H. J. Minnemeyer, the lead researcher on the company''s nicotine augmentation programs, updated Spears on the progress of efforts to solve "the problem of delivering more nicotine in the smoke of low tar cigarettes." The memorandum described the various research projects being conducted by Defendants as well as the medical and scientific research articles that had been published on the subject. 95539652-9655 (US 56825).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'm-s-ireland-titled-research', '468', 'M. S. Ireland, titled "Research Proposal -- Development of Assay for Free Nicotine," again acknowledged the scientific consensus that nicotine was the source of the addiction to smoking:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', '82396938-6939-6938-us-22012', '469', '82396938-6939 at 6938 (US 22012).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'project-according-november-9-1976', '469', 'Project." According to a November 9, 1976 memorandum from company Vice President for'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'marketing-r-e-smith-research', '469', 'Marketing R.E. Smith to the research department, the project was abandoned. However, the memorandum disclosed that, despite Lorillard''s contrary public declarations, the company was well aware of the importance of nicotine in sustaining smoking:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', '01244504-4504-us-20042', '469', '01244504-4504 (US 20042).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', '00110371-0371-us-34404', '470', '00110371-0371 (US 34404).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'information-task-force-memorandum-provided', '470', 'Information Task Force." The memorandum provided Lorillard executives, including Dr. Spears, details of the secret project:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', '01394380-4381-4380-us-21543', '470', '01394380-4381 at 4380 (US 21543).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'undoubtedly-nicotine-serves-primary-role', '470', '"Undoubtedly, nicotine serves a primary role in cigarette smoking." He also noted that, "A real problem in this whole area is the diversity of terms employed to say essentially the same thing." He later stated that:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', '01105000-5021-5001-5002-5009-us', '471', '01105000-5021 at 5001, 5002, 5009 (US 20030).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'titrated-achieve-greater-nicotine-dose', '471', '("titrated") to achieve a greater "nicotine dose":'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'id-5010', '471', 'Id. at 5010.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'f-american-tobacco-company', '471', 'f. American Tobacco Company'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'lucky-strike-other-leading-brands', '471', 'Lucky Strike and Other Leading Brands of Cigarettes." The document contained a section called'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'importance-nicotine-tobacco-tobacco-products', '472', '"Importance of Nicotine in Tobacco and Tobacco Products." Hanmer observed that the following'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'facts-long-common-knowledge-company', '472', '"facts" were "long common knowledge" at the company:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'atx300006425-6468v-6428-us-85324', '472', 'ATX300006425-6468V at 6428 (US 85324).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'acted-like-traditional-pharmaceutical-companies', '473', 'acted like traditional pharmaceutical companies," studying the pharmacokinetics (absorption, metabolism, and excretion) of nicotine, the pharmacodynamics (effects on body chemistry) of nicotine, and the clinical effectiveness (whether drug is effective in producing the desired effects) of nicotine. VX1242326-3211 at 2806-2087 (US 64323).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', 'l-stone-summarizes-significance-ctr-funded', '475', 'L. Stone, summarizes the significance of CTR-funded nicotine research for industry clients. In his memorandum, Stone conceded that:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'MANNER', '515709297-9340-9298-us-20866', '475', '515709297-9340 at 9298 (US 20866).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'do-so', '475', 'Do So'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'these-statements-are-detailed-below', '476', 'These statements are detailed below.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'regulation-tobacco-products-part-i', '478', 'Regulation of Tobacco Products (Part I) Hearings before the Subcommittee on Health and the Environment of the Committee on Energy and Commerce, House of Representatives, 103rd Congress April 14, 1994, 2023195738-5892 (US 21990).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'facts-you-should-know-one', '479', '"FACTS YOU SHOULD KNOW." One of the "facts" was that "Philip Morris does not believe cigarette smoking is addictive. People can and do quit all the time." 2023011263-1263 (US 20371).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'myron-levin-jury-views-ceo', '480', 'Myron Levin, Jury Views CEO''s "Gummy Bear" Tobacco Deposition: Philip Morris Executive'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'testifies-cigarettes-aren-t-any', '480', 'Testifies Cigarettes Aren''t Any More Addictive Than Coffee or Candy," Los Angeles Times, July'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', '18-1997-d3-morgan-pd', '480', '18, 1997, at D3; Morgan PD, Broin v. Phillip Morris, et al., 4/17/97, 77:20-78:23.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'statement-position-issued-pmc-letterhead', '480', 'Statement of Position), issued on PMC letterhead, Philip Morris once again denied addiction and claimed that cigarettes were addictive only under definitional changes that can be used to "describe many different kinds of behavior." However, Philip Morris did agree to cease all public debate on the issue. 2063123083-3084 (US 39734).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'bible-admitted-philip-morris-companies', '480', 'Bible admitted that Philip Morris Companies'' position was "at odds . . . with the public health community," and said that for the sake of a consistent public health message, Philip Morris'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'companies-would-no-longer-debate', '480', 'Companies would no longer debate the addictiveness of nicotine except insofar as it was "necessary to defend ourselves and our opinions in the courts." 83623323-3347 at 3343-3344 (US 21820).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', '1982-rjr-chairman-ceo-edward', '481', '1982, RJR Chairman and CEO Edward Horrigan stated under oath that "with regard to addiction, there is absolutely no proof that cigarettes are addictive." At the time of this statement, Horrigan was also chairman of the Tobacco Institute executive committee. 521056398-6557 at 6411 (US 85334); 201830983-0993 (US 36327).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'regulation-tobacco-products-part-i-2', '482', 'Regulation of Tobacco Products (Part I) Hearings before the Subcommittee on Health and the Environment of the Committee on Energy and Commerce, House of Representatives, 103rd Congress April 14, 1994, 2023195738-5892 at 5780-5781 (US 36975).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', '525412344-2488-2374-us-88563', '483', '525412344-2488 at 2374 (US 88563).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'document-added', '485', 'The document added that'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'rjr-s-position-addiction-failed', '485', 'RJR''s position on addiction failed to mention nicotine at all. 522879046-9047 at 9046 (US 85340).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'bat-industries-director-batco-martin', '486', 'BAT Industries and Director of BATCo, Martin Broughton, denied any concealment of research linking smoking and addiction, saying that, "We have no internal research which proves that . . . smoking is addictive." State of Minnesota v. Philip Morris, Inc., et al., C1-94-8565, Exhibit No. 2909, 700428854-8856 at 8854 (US 85342). This statement, made to analysts, investors, and journalists, was confirmed in a BATCo "Company Notice" to employees dated October 31, 1996.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', '2070052572-2578-2577-us-87151', '487', '2070052572-2578 at 2577 (US 87151).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', '536502262-2266-2262-us-20930', '487', '536502262-2266 at 2262 (US 20930).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'boyse-blackie-stated-company-s', '487', 'Boyse (Blackie), stated the company''s public position on addiction:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', '500810940-0941-0940-us-23036', '488', '500810940-0941 at 0940 (US 23036).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'id-0941', '488', 'Id. at 0941.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', '321007259-7260-us-85344', '488', '321007259-7260 (US 85344).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'testified-under-penalty-perjury-nationally', '489', 'testified under penalty of perjury in a nationally televised hearing before the House Subcommittee on Health and the Environment. During this hearing, Johnston denied that nicotine is addictive:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'regulation-tobacco-products-part-i-3', '489', 'Regulation of Tobacco Products (Part I) Hearings before the Subcommittee on Health and the Environment of the Committee on Energy and Commerce, House of Representatives, 103rd Congress April 14, 1994, 2023195738-5892 at 5780-5781 (US 21990).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'company-understood-industry-s-free', '489', 'company understood that the industry''s "free choice" argument in litigation would be undermined by any suggestion that smoking and nicotine were addictive. In the words of long-time general counsel Ernest Pepples, in a February 14, 1973 "Confidential" memorandum to public relations director John Ballock, one of the "salient problems now facing the cigarette industry" was:');
INSERT INTO passages (report, section, paragraph_id, page, body) VALUES
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'regulation-tobacco-products-part-i-4', '490', 'Regulation of Tobacco Products (Part I) Hearings before the Subcommittee on Health and the Environment of the Committee on Energy and Commerce, House of Representatives, 103rd Congress April 14, 1994, 2023195738-5892 at 5780-5781 (US 21990).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'surgeon-general-1988-cigarette-smoking', '491', 'Surgeon General in 1988, cigarette smoking would be classified as addictive," it went on to state that:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'response-brown-williamson-tobacco-corporation', '491', 'Response of Brown & Williamson Tobacco Corporation to the United States'' First Set of Requests for Admission to All Defendants, RFA# 390, April 19, 2002, USX6390001-0400 at 0094 (US 89555).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'testified-under-penalty-perjury-nationally-2', '491', 'testified under penalty of perjury in a nationally televised hearing before the House of Representatives Subcommittee on Health and the Environment. During this hearing, Tisch also denied that nicotine is addictive:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'regulation-tobacco-products-part-i-5', '491', 'Regulation of Tobacco Products (Part I) Hearings before the Subcommittee on Health and the Environment of the Committee on Energy and Commerce, House of Representatives, 103rd Congress April 14, 1994, 2023195738-5892 at 5780-5781 (US 21990).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'inc-edward-horrigan-formerly-rjr', '493', 'Inc., Edward A. Horrigan (formerly of RJR), also testified under penalty of perjury in a nationally televised hearing before the House of Representatives Subcommittee on Health and the Environment. During this hearing, Horrigan denied that nicotine is addictive:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'regulation-tobacco-products-part-i-6', '493', 'Regulation of Tobacco Products (Part I) Hearings before the Subcommittee on Health and the Environment of the Committee on Energy and Commerce, House of Representatives, 103rd Congress April 14, 1994, 2023195738-5892 at 5780-5781 (US 21990).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'industry-s-campaign-deny-addiction', '493', 'industry''s campaign to deny addiction and conceal internal research and knowledge. Over the decades, the Tobacco Institute, on behalf of the cigarette company Defendants, publicly disseminated countless false, deceptive, or misleading statements denying the addictiveness of nicotine and cigarette smoking. The following instances exemplify the many statements made by TI employees in the organization''s attempts to deceive and mislead the American public about smoking, the role nicotine played in smoking, why quitting was so difficult, and whether people who could not quit smoking merely lacked will-power.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Publicly Denied that Nicotine Is Addictive and Continue to', 'timn0019963-9963-us-21239-dawson-wd', '497', 'TIMN0019963-9963 (US 21239); Dawson WD, 38:18-23.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'press-release-also-attacked-surgeon', '498', 'This press release also attacked the Surgeon General''s Report and specifically denied any dependence on nicotine, stating:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'timn0019964-9965-9964-us-85366', '498', 'TIMN0019964-9965 at 9964 (US 85366).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'covington-burling-lawyers-several-defendants', '499', 'Covington & Burling lawyers and several Defendants prior to being delivered to Congress. Instead, the press releases implied that both scientists testified independently before the House Subcommittee. TIMN0125189-5189 (US 77065); 506419103-9103 (US 85372); 87701893-1903 (US 32065); 2025875995-5997 (US 85375).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'timn341405-1422-1420-us-21363', '499', 'TIMN341405-1422 at 1420 (US 21363).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'surgeon-general-nearly-half-all', '500', 'Surgeon General, nearly half of all Americans who ever smoked have quit and most of the 41 million smokers who quit did so without formal treatment programs or smoking cessation devices."'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'timn341503-1504-us-85377', '500', 'TIMN341503-1504 (US 85377).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'corti1731-1738-us-87735', '500', 'CORTI1731-1738 (US 87735).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'blau-had-once-again-testified', '500', 'Blau had once again testified before a House Subcommittee denying the addictiveness of cigarettes.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'tobacco-institute-provided-following-its', '500', 'The Tobacco Institute provided the following in its press release, once again omitting any mention of the tie between Blau and the cigarette manufacturer Defendants:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'timn0026755-6757-6757-us-85379', '501', 'TIMN0026755-6757 at 6757 (US 85379).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'id-0459-0460-dawson-wd-45', '502', 'Id. at 0459-0460; Dawson WD, 45:7-48:23.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'advisory-board-told-congressional-subcommittee', '503', 'Advisory Board, told a Congressional subcommittee in hearings held in April 1969 that "smoking tobacco is not considered an addiction." 500925974-5998 at 5976 (US 85382); BWX0007189-7297 (US 36237).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'overwhelming-evidence-smoking-addictive-no', '504', 'overwhelming evidence that smoking is addictive, no Defendant accepts the Surgeon General''s definition of addiction, no Defendant admits that nicotine is the drug delivered by cigarettes that creates and sustains addiction, and no Defendant acknowledges that the reason quitting smoking is so difficult, and not simply a function of individual will power, is because of its addictive nature.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', '"CLAIMS THAT CIGARETTES ARE ADDICTIVE IRRESPONSIBLE AND SCARE TACTICS."', 'tlt1020158-0158-us-87157-ivey-wd', '505', 'TLT1020158-0158 (US 87157); Ivey WD, 94:16-96:1.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', 'understanding-nicotine-its-role-creating', '509', 'understanding of nicotine and its role in creating smoking addiction -- an understanding that is totally inconsistent with their long-standing public denials that nicotine is addictive. In addition, it is clear that Defendants intentionally withheld from public dissemination, from the public health community, and from government authorities, accurate and important information regarding the addictiveness of nicotine in cigarettes. Henningfield WD, 87:10-88:20, 161:23-167:6.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', 'timn0107822-7823-7823-us-21275', '510', 'TIMN0107822-7823 at 7823 (US 21275).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', '1000046538-6546-6543-us-26074-2021423403-3497', '512', '1000046538-6546 at 6543 (US 26074); 2021423403-3497 at 3485 (US 36743).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', '1003726420-6420-us-85384-emphasis-original', '513', '1003726420-6420 (US 85384) (emphasis in original).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', '2021424402-4412-4404-4405-us-22847', '516', '2021424402-4412 at 4404-4405 (US 22847).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', '2021424402-4412-4412-us-22847-2021423403-3461', '516', '2021424402-4412 at 4412 (US 22847); 2021423403-3461 at 3422 (US 87038*).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', 'denoble-explained-his-testimony-before', '517', 'DeNoble explained in his testimony before the Waxman Subcommittee in 1994:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', '2023963269-3341-3285-us-20398-assessment', '517', '2023963269-3341 at 3285 (US 20398). This assessment echoes Dr. Henningfield''s view about the adverse impact that Philip Morris''s concealment had on the scientific research community.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', 'henningfield-wd-162-10-167-6', '517', 'Henningfield WD, 162:10-167:6.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', '2023963269-3341-3305-3306-us-20398-see', '519', '2023963269-3341 at 3305-3306 (US 20398); See also, DeNoble WD, 38:12-14.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', 'denoble-wd-39-12-42-20', '520', 'DeNoble WD, 39:12-42:20; Mele WD, 28:13-30:22; 2047340350-0350 (US 22772); 2077541354- 1354 (US 44603).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Concealed and Suppressed Research Data and Other', '2046819241-9268-9249-9250-us-85265', '521', '2046819241-9268 at 9249-9250 (US 85265*).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '2046819241-9268-9256--9257-us-85265', '522', '2046819241-9268 at 9256 -9257 (US 85265*) (citations omitted).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'nicorette-philip-morris-understood-threat', '523', 'Nicorette. Philip Morris understood the threat Nicorette posed, namely allowing nicotine-addicted smokers to quit, and took action:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '2023799799-9800-9799-us-26801', '523', '2023799799-9800 at 9799 (US 26801).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'see-also-2023799804-9804-us-26802', '523', 'See also 2023799804-9804 (US 26802); 2023799803-9803 (US 37049).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'b-batco', '523', 'b. BATCo'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'english-equivalent-ctr-sir-charles', '523', 'English equivalent of CTR, Sir Charles Ellis enclosed several nicotine research reports, but stated that the BATCo Board "wish me to ask you that these reports should be kept strictly confidential" by the TRC executive committee. Ellis''s letter is marked "STRICTLY PERSONAL AND CONFIDENTIAL." 105620759-0761 (US 85387).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '109840698-0702-0699-0700-us-21733-emphasis', '525', '109840698-0702 at 0699-0700 (US 21733) (emphasis in original).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'batco-rjr-concealed-nicotine-addiction', '525', 'BATCo and RJR, concealed nicotine and addiction research from the Surgeon General''s Advisory Committee, which was then in the process of preparing the Surgeon General''s 1984 Report on smoking and health.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '680249785-9786-us-85391', '527', '680249785-9786 (US 85391)).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'batco-report-from-dr-haselbach', '528', 'BATCo report from Dr. Haselbach titled "A Tentative Hypothesis on Nicotine Addiction." MTP0013569-3569 (US 76159).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '689033419-3419-us-54271', '528', '689033419-3419 (US 54271).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '689033422-3422-us-22734', '529', '689033422-3422 (US 22734).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'great-concern-about-ferris-s', '532', '"great concern" about Ferris''s report because it conceded that smoking is addictive. Pepples did not deny any of Ferris'' observations or conclusions regarding nicotine and smoking; indeed, he admitted that Ferris'' use of authorities and references was "generally accurate." Fearing involvement by the'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'fda-pepples-then-recommended-closer', '532', 'FDA, Pepples then recommended closer involvement by lawyers in scientific projects in the future.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'he-wrote', '532', 'He wrote:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '521016786-6786-us-22129', '533', '521016786-6786 (US 22129).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'november-12-1984-letter-bat', '533', 'November 12, 1984 letter to BAT attorney Anne Johnson why the BAT study must not be publicized:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '680583045-3045-us-85395', '533', '680583045-3045 (US 85395).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'anomalies-bat-scientist-dr-l', '533', 'Anomalies" by BAT scientist Dr. L.C.F. Blackman. By letter dated October 25, 1984, B&W attorney J. Kendrick Wells wrote BATCo counsel Alec Morini that "review" of BATCo publications by B&W was necessary in light of ongoing smoking and health litigation; Wells went on to provide'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '45-paragraphs-revisions-blackman-s', '533', '45 paragraphs of revisions to Blackman''s draft report in a marked-up report These included:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '202337394-7394-us-21965', '534', '202337394-7394 (US 21965).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'example-september-16-1938-letter', '534', 'example, in a September 16, 1938 letter, H.R. Hanmer of American''s R & D Department informed George W. Hill, an American Vice President, that research performed on dogs had demonstrated an increase in blood pressure due to the cigarette''s nicotine. Mr. Hanmer added that while this was "very clear-cut biological evidence," "nothing of this sort could ever be used in presenting facts to the public." MNAT00115492-5499 (US 21401).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'addiction-research-would-not-be', '535', 'addiction research would not be performed. In a May 17, 1983 memorandum from Fred Panzer to David Henderson, Panzer wrote that certain legislation needed to be amended to make it favorable to the industry by preventing addiction research: "We need language for a rider to the appropriation bill for the National Institute on Drug Abuse (NIDA) that would prevent the use of appropriated funds for a study of the addictive properties of tobacco." TIME 370968-0968 (US 62769).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'industry-see-sections-iii-c', '535', 'industry. See Sections III(C)(2) and III(E)(2-3), supra. Lawyers played an important part in carefully considering all nicotine and "psychopharmacology" research before it was started to insure it would stay "pro-industry." Id. Nicotine/behavioral research was discussed by the CTR Director and industry counsel at a July 28, 1976 meeting of the industry Research Liaison Committee, where long-time industry counsel Dave Hardy (of Shook, Hardy & Bacon), American Tobacco counsel Cyril Hetsko, and others commented:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '1003719175-9179-us-86406', '536', '1003719175-9179 (US 86406).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'anecdotal-evidence-nicotine-administration-rats', '537', '"anecdotal evidence" that nicotine administration in rats leads to withdrawal symptoms. The conference and the book were sponsored by CTR. LD90011031-0330 (US 87320).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '1000041912-1918-1912-us-20073', '537', '1000041912-1918 at 1912 (US 20073).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'ford-s-nicotine-research-which', '538', 'Ford''s nicotine research, which included the goal of finding a "nicotine blocker or substitute," was'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'mere-speculation-nonetheless-responded-follows', '538', '"mere speculation," she nonetheless responded as follows:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '968148608-8639-us-88840', '538', '968148608-8639 (US 88840).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'recited-may-10-1978-notes', '538', 'As recited in the May 10, 1978 notes of the Industry Technical Committee Chairman Preston Leake'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'scientific-director-american-arnold-henson', '538', '(scientific director for American) to Arnold Henson (General Counsel for American), the proposed nicotine work was "ruled out" by outside counsel Ed Jacob. 955017148-7154 at 7149-7150 (US'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '87172', '538', '87172).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '100651251-1312-1299-us-85402', '540', '100651251-1312 at 1299 (US 85402*).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '100651251-1312-1299-1300-us-85402', '540', '100651251-1312 at 1299-1300 (US 85402*).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '1000138177-8234-8219-us-87174', '541', '1000138177-8234 at 8219 (US 87174).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', '1000138177-8234-8221-us-87174', '542', '1000138177-8234 at 8221 (US 87174).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'denied-addictiveness-smoking-nicotine-s', '542', 'denied the addictiveness of smoking and nicotine''s central role in smoking. They made these denials out of fear that public acknowledgment of what was so well documented and widely accepted internally within their corporate offices and scientific laboratories could result in governmental (i.e., FDA) regulation, adverse liability judgments from addicted smokers suffering the adverse health effects of smoking, loss of social acceptability of smoking, and the ultimate loss of corporate profits.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Why Was Research Stopped', 'evidence-spelled-out-above-simply', '543', 'The evidence spelled out above is simply overwhelming that Defendants knew that smoking is addictive and knew that nicotine is the agent creating and sustaining that addiction. There is also overwhelming evidence that even though Defendants have known internally about addiction for decades, they have endeavored to keep the extensive research and data they had accumulated out of the public domain and out of the hands of the public health community by denying that such data existed, by refusing to disclose it, and by shutting down or censoring laboratories and research projects which were investigating the mechanisms of nicotine.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Nicotine "Manipulation": Defendants Have Falsely Denied That They Can and', 'creates-sustains-addiction-smoking-cigarette', '545', 'creates and sustains an addiction to smoking and that cigarette sales, and ultimately tobacco company profits, depend on creating and sustaining that addiction. Section V(B)(3), supra. Given the importance of nicotine to the ultimate financial health of Defendants, they have undertaken extensive research into how nicotine operates within the human body and how the physical and chemical design parameters of cigarettes influence the delivery of nicotine to smokers. Using the knowledge produced by that research, Defendants have designed their cigarettes to precisely control nicotine delivery levels and provide doses of nicotine sufficient to create and sustain addiction. At the same time, Defendants have concealed much of their nicotine-related research, and have continuously and vigorously denied their efforts to control nicotine levels and delivery.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'often-referred-optimum-amount-nicotine', '547', 'often referred to as an "optimum" amount of nicotine: one that would meet smokers'' demand for lower nicotine and tar products, while still providing enough nicotine to create and sustain addiction. Defendants'' efforts often centered on attempting to identify a particular dose of nicotine that would "satisfy" smokers'' need for nicotine and thereby assure continued smoking.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '51170312103124-us-51575-500994992-4941-4934', '549', '51170312103124 (US 51575); 500994992-4941 at 4934 (US 85405); 504223322-3327 at 3322 (US 50614); 536000308-0507 at 0334 (US 85298); 602759-2759 (US 53297).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'amount-nicotine-cigarettes-since-early', '549', 'amount of nicotine in cigarettes since as early as the 1940s, when it first conducted studies on controlling nicotine in its Parliament cigarettes. In a 1954 document titled, "An Outline of Current and Proposed Quality Control, for example, Development and Research for Benson and Hedges," Philip Morris discussed its program to "ensur[e] the over-all quality and uniformity of the finished Parliament cigarette and thus maintain[] its appeal for discriminating smokers." The program was focused on "nicotine content, tar content, acid-base balance, and content of taste and aroma factors." The memorandum described Philip Morris''s efforts to use "informally constituted smoking panels and the results of nicotine analyses performed on the blends . . . to establish a desirable level of nicotine concentration in the blend and hence also in the smoke." 1001761472-1484 at 1472-73 (US'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'smokers-504210018-0018-us-50577', '551', 'smokers. 504210018-0018 (US 50577).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '505243357-3365-3357-3359-3360-3362', '552', '505243357-3365 at 3357, 3359, 3360, 3362 and 3365 (US 86944).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'laurene-described-reynolds-s-strategies', '552', 'Laurene described Reynolds''s strategies of maintaining "physiological satisfaction" in low tar cigarettes as follows:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '---improve-method-control-ph', '552', '-- Improve method to control pH and free nicotine in smoke.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'laurene-reported-efforts-are-already', '552', 'Laurene reported that "efforts are already underway on each of these approaches to improve nicotine delivery and impact." 500884922-4941 at 4933-34 (US 85405); see also 502314530-4547 at 4538'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'us-21917-502967936-7944-7944-us', '552', '(US 21917); 502967936-7944 at 7944 (US 76188).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'd-h-piehl-wrote-alan', '552', 'D.H. Piehl wrote to Alan Rodgman that an objective of the 1977-1978 research was to "[d]efine the optimum nicotine level in cigarette smoke required to maximize smoker satisfaction. Determine the existence of a minimum or threshold value of nicotine required for satisfaction." 504423322-3327 at 3322 (US 50614) (emphasis in original). In addition, the document clearly distinguished between taste and satisfaction. Id.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '511703121-3121-us-51575-see-also', '555', '511703121-3121 (US 51575). See also 511223463-3484 (US 20840).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '9117-us-20112-early-1960s', '555', '9117 (US 20112). By the early 1960s, BATCo and B&W scientists were confident that they could design cigarettes to deliver optimum doses of nicotine. A September 16, 1963 letter to BATCo from Robert Griffith, Director of Research and Development at B&W, discussed "optimum levels" for nicotine and correlated the nicotine level in cigarettes with consumer acceptance. The letter recognized that the nicotine levels of B&W cigarettes were not obtained "by accident" and admitted that "we have a research program in progress to obtain . . . any level of nicotine desired." 102630333-0336 at 0334, 0335-0336 (US 23000).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '602759-2759-us-53297', '556', '602759-2759 (US 53297).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'throughout-1970s-part-its-nicotine', '556', 'throughout the 1970s. As part of its Nicotine Augmentation Project, Lorillard conducted an extensive review of the literature on the "pharmacology of smoke-dose nicotine with the goal of discovering some indications of threshold dose and optimum doses of nicotine in average cigarette smokers." In a December 10, 1976 report relating to the project, H.S. Tong, a scientist at the Lorillard Research Center, recommended that, in light of Lorillard''s knowledge of smoker compensation,"[i]t would seem desirable to have a low tar cigarette with a nicotine content between the threshold and optimum doses level." Tong also set forth the results of Lorillard''s extensive review of scientific literature on the subject of determining the optimum nicotine dose and summarized, "[n]o single parameter appears to offer a reliable handle for measuring optimum satisfaction dose of nicotine at the present time. . . . In a subjective study, test subjects reported that they found cigarettes of 0.8 mg to be acceptable." 00045061-5071 at 5061, 5063, 5068 (US 34210); Henningfield WD, 94:6-8, 97:6-12; Farone WD, 132:18-133:12.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'liggett-s-various-ongoing-research', '557', 'Liggett''s various ongoing research and product development projects reported that "[w]e are making progress in adjusting tar and nicotine yields where necessary on brands utilizing the consolidated blend," and "[w]e are still working with paper suppliers to find an appropriate paper to put us exactly on target on Full Flavor Generic tar and nicotine delivery." LWDOJ9272790-1791 at 2790 (US 86947) (Confidential).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'important-formulating-successful-cigarette-strategy', '558', 'was important in formulating a successful cigarette strategy. A February 9, 1960 memorandum from L.L. Long, Research and Development Engineer, to A.B. Clarke, Research and Development Scientist, and copied to Robert Seligman, who became Vice President of Research and Development, discussed Philip Morris''s ongoing efforts to control the amount of nicotine received by a smoker through concentration of nicotine in tobacco smoke while maintaining lower levels of tar. Long wrote:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '1001919958-9958-us-85460-issue-received', '558', '1001919958-9958 (US 85460). This issue received further attention throughout 1960. 1001919941- 9941 (US 21753).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '1004863921-9975-3921-3922-us-35921-1000356550-6576', '562', '1004863921-9975 at 3921-3922 (US 35921); 1000356550-6576 (US 85425); 0000258064-8064 (US 85426).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '1003288934-8949-8943-us-20165-studies', '563', '1003288934-8949 at 8943 (US 20165). Studies involving nicotine citrate continued into 1977. 1003285418-5418 (US 85427); 2081925847-5856 at 5851 (US 88784).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '2022150943-0951-0947-us-85254-dr', '563', '2022150943-0951 at 0947 (US 85254). Dr. Jerry Whidby, Philip Morris scientist from 1972 to 1998 and presently a paid consultant for Philip Morris, who held the title of Senior Fellow, the highest non-management scientific position at Philip Morris, acknowledged that nicotine deliveries identified in this memorandum "show[] almost uniformly that the brands with the lower FTC tar deliveries actually have higher nicotine to tar ratios." Whidby TT, 2/22/05, 14015:8-14016:14.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'another-accomplishment-described-research-showing', '564', 'Another accomplishment they described was research showing that "there are optimal cigarette nicotine deliveries for producing the most favorable physiological and behavioral responses," and that "all forms of nicotine are not behaviorally or physiologically equal." 2028813366-3368 at 3366, 3367-3368 (US 20430); Henningfield WD, 102:2-10.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'would-deliver-high-level-nicotine', '565', 'that would deliver a high level of nicotine, in order to have a competitive advantage in the marketplace. In a May 19, 1972 internal memorandum to Claude Teague from Frank G. Colby, Manager of Scientific Information, Colby recounted a conversation he had with an employee of Imperial Tobacco Company while preparing for an industry-wide conference:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'colby-also-reported-his-suspicion', '566', 'Colby also reported his suspicion that Philip Morris already was developing a high nicotine cigarette. Colby concluded: "I feel these two incidents prove that the high nicotine tar ratio cigarette is a concept which is ''very much in the air.'' We should definitely make an effort to be first." 500790798-0798 (US 20651); see also 500901543-1545 (US 85428).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '500790776-0784-0776-0777-us-29473-emphasis', '566', '500790776-0784 at 0776-0777 (US 29473) (emphasis in original).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '501166152-6153-6152-us-23051-emphasis', '567', '501166152-6153 at 6152 (US 23051) (emphasis in original); Henningfield WD, 75:22-76:1, 78:1,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '79-16-20', '567', '79:16-20.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '500884613-4638-4619-4620-us-85430-see', '568', '500884613-4638 at 4619-4620 (US 85430); see also 504707350-7372 at 7365 (US 86950); 504424968-4976 (US 86951).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'rest-reestablishment-solubles-tobacco-process', '572', '"REST" [Reestablishment of Solubles in Tobacco] process. A December 11, 1989 internal memorandum from Rhenda H. Steele to Barry S. Fagg explained the purpose of REST processing as follows: "Using this process, all variables [in tobacco] are held constant except nicotine, the concentration of which can be controlled." 509887264-7264 (US 85437). A May 3, report titled "REST Program Review" explained Reynolds''s goal for use of REST processing to control nicotine delivery in its products:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '509479574-9587-9854-us-20829-henningfield', '572', '509479574-9587 at 9854 (US 20829); Henningfield WD, 102:2-4, 102:11-12.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '508112783-2807-2788-2790-2792-2794', '573', '508112783-2807 at 2788, 2790, 2792, 2794 (US 85438). An August 22, 1990 report on REST technology stated that Reynolds had "achieved on a development scale a practical process for selectively adjusting the nicotine content in a tobacco extract and, therefore, in the tobacco blend after REST processing" and that Reynolds intended to use REST technology to "[i]ndependently control nicotine delivery, from very low to elevated levels." 509479574-9587 at 9576, 9577 (US 20829); see also 509479799-9799 (US 86958); 508099588-9588 (US 86959); 507051133-1136 (US 86960); 512331601-1601 (US 86961*).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '525246100-6103-6100-us-85442', '574', '525246100-6103 at 6100 (US 85442).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '509348227-8229-8228-us-88072', '575', '509348227-8229 at 8228 (US 88072).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'fagg-concluded-t-overall-process', '575', 'Fagg concluded that "[t]he overall process has demonstrated the ability to start with lamina cut filler and produce processed tobacco cut filler in which the nicotine level within the tobacco can be controlled," and that "[c]ontrolled nicotine materials allow novel manipulations of tar to nicotine ratios which significantly alter smoking sensations. Product opportunities are apparent and consumer testing has been included in the Winston and Vantage brand families." 508380001-0027 at 0008, 0014-0015 (US 85443).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '1990s-company-concentrated-research-would', '575', '1990s. The company concentrated on research that would allow it to create an ultra low tar product that provided nicotine delivery similar to full-flavor products. 509308455-8459 (US 20827).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'admitted-b-w-absolutely-had', '576', 'admitted that B&W "absolutely" had the ability to manipulate the nicotine to tar ratio of its products. Honeycutt PD, United States v. Philip Morris, 4/23/02, 160:15-19.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'counsel-addison-yeaman-evaluated-findings', '577', 'Counsel Addison Yeaman evaluated the findings of HIPPO I and II, and suggested that the best reaction to the Surgeon General''s Report was to provide a filter capable of removing certain constituents of smoke considered suspect by public health officials, while still "delivering full flavor -- and incidentally -- a nice jolt of nicotine." 2046754905-4909 at 4908-4909 (US 20477).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'low-tar-ultra-low-tar', '578', '"Low Tar" and "Ultra Low Tar" products was higher than for "Full Flavor" products. 5050034311458. BATCo held a Nicotine Conference in England, from June 6-8, 1984. Session titles included: "A smokers [sic] requirement for nicotine. A smoking behavior and market place view;" "Product elasticity, nicotine and perception of product strength;" and "Product modification for maximal nicotine effects." Two out of the three "primary objectives" of the Conference were:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '512106427-6437-6428-29-6430-us-20846', '578', '512106427-6437 at 6428-29, 6430 (US 20846).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '109869437-9440-9437-9438-us-21707', '579', '109869437-9440 at 9437-9438 (US 21707).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '400854060-4066-4060-us-67851-baker', '580', '400854060-4066 at 4060 (US 67851). Baker noted that since the 1960s, B&W conducted such nicotine delivery research as part of a coordinated BAT Group research strategy, with "work done by a variety of groups in Southampton, Louisville and elsewhere." Id.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '201752783-2899-2837-2838-us-85452', '581', '201752783-2899 at 2837, 2838 (US 85452).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'sensory-effects-changing-tar-nicotine', '581', '"sensory effects of changing tar/nicotine ratios of smoke." The summary was devised to help'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'point-way-toward-manipulat-ing', '581', '"point[] the way" toward "manipulat[ing] and modify[ing] the impact and irritation sensations and the balance between them." The summary pointed out the following main conclusions:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '4-nicotine-source-impact', '581', '(4) nicotine is the source of impact.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'review-states-batco-s-research', '581', 'The Review states that BATCo''s research was aimed at gaining an "understanding of how impact and irritation can be tailored to meet product requirements by optimisation of nicotine delivery."'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '575102998-3015-3002-3003-3010-us-53213', '581', '575102998-3015 at 3002-3003, 3010 (US 53213).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'nicotine-tar-ratio-naturally-found', '582', 'nicotine to tar ratio naturally found in cigarettes. These methods included adding nicotine to reconstituted tobacco, increasing the amount of Burley tobacco -- which is naturally higher in nicotine content than other tobaccos -- in a blend to determine its effect on the "nicotine yield," and producing tobacco plants, such as N. Rustica, that had almost double the concentration of nicotine of other tobacco plants used by American. MNAT00316688-6693 (US 21219); X003421-3431 at 3421, 3424 (US 34158); MNAT00881318-1323 (US 21221); see also X002999-3038 (US 88082).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'also-found-compound-w-added', '583', 'It was also found that "Compound W added to tobacco has little effect on the overall taste of the cigarette. Impact is increased slightly." ATX050142123-2128 at 2123-2124 (US 21602); Sprinkle PD, Carter, 1/19/96, 99:16-17; Henningfield WD, 93:9-94:5.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'describes-lorillard-s-research-efforts', '583', 'describes Lorillard''s research efforts:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'spears-further-stated-would-be', '584', 'Spears further stated that "it would be useful to have a wider range of control over nicotine than now exists, through the selection of tobacco and the physical construction of the smoking article. Within one year, it is expected that tobacco modifications can be made to optimize absorption of smoke nicotine." 87667736-7740 at 7737 (US 56302*). In an undated paper, titled "Factors Affecting Smoke Delivery of Nicotine and Carbon Monoxide," Spears identified several factors that affect the nicotine yield of a cigarette and stated: "Through [a] combination of these variables, plant genetics and commercial processes, . . . it is possible to manipulate the yield of nicotine from about .1 mg to 4 mg per cigarette." 00044998-5021 at 5001-5002 (US 34208).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'minnemeyer-schultz-re-nicotine-augmentation', '586', 'Minnemeyer to Schultz re Nicotine Augmentation Project (NAP), US 47721 (at 1). This project is discussed further in Section V(C)(1)(a)(4).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '00044525-4528-4525-4526-us-34191', '587', '00044525-4528 at 4525, 4526 (US 34191).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '00044955-4970-4959-us-85459-farone', '588', '00044955-4970 at 4959 (US 85459); Farone WD, 96:7-19.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'liggett-s-new-product-concepts', '590', 'Liggett''s new product concepts included producing a "low tar cigarette with increased nicotine impact." Methods for designing this proposed cigarette included adding nicotine to the tobacco blend, or increasing the smoke pH through the use of additives. LWDOJ9165472-5472 (US 22169).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'omni-quest-cigarette-brands-purpose', '591', 'Omni and Quest cigarette brands, "The purpose of the samples that have been tested on these two products is to see if we can maintain taste/impact while lowering the tar." VDOJ 25299-5299 (US 75450) (Confidential). In another e-mail dated August 27, 2001, Jackson informed Liggett and Vector management that the Omni was being modified in order to "attempt to provide more initial impact to the cigarette." Jackson further informed management that alternatives were being considered "to try and moderately increase nicotine." VDOJ 25348-5348 (US 64735) (Confidential).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'commercial-success-any-cigarette-depends', '591', 'commercial success of any cigarette depends on its ability to deliver adequate levels of nicotine to smokers. As the internal documents discussed below reveal, each Defendant also understood that its market position, as well as the financial viability of the tobacco industry as a whole, required the development of cigarettes that provide nicotine in amounts sufficient to ensure that smokers become and remain addicted. Accordingly, each Defendant took steps, over a sustained period of time, to develop such cigarettes.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'marketing-planning-rjr-reported-study', '592', 'of Marketing and Planning at RJR, reported that a study conducted by Reynolds''s Marketing and Development Department found a direct, significant correlation between the amount of "free nicotine" in a brand and the sales level of that brand. 501011401-1401 (US 20668). Blevins summarized the results of the study and his recommendations in a July 12, 1973 memorandum to William S. Smith, Jr., member of the CTR Research Board of Directors, as follows:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '501011403-1403-us-20669-free-nicotine', '593', '501011403-1403 (US 20669). Free nicotine is nicotine in the unprotonated form, i.e., without a proton or positive chemical charge. See also definition and discussion of "free nicotine" at V(C)(2)(d)(1); see also Farone WD, 93:22-98:21.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '500606138-6153-6138-us-48334-similarly', '593', '500606138-6153 at 6138 (US 48334). Similarly, in 1982, Reynolds reported internally that shortly after Philip Morris began increasing smoke pH and free nicotine through the introduction of added ammonia (diammonium hydrogen phosphate) in 1965, Philip Morris''s sales began growing very rapidly. 500540827-0832 (US 20639).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'batco-s-chemist-john-kirwan', '594', 'BATCo''s Chemist, John Kirwan, discussed "the question of desirable or optimum levels for either nicotine or sugar or a balance between the two," and how the level of nicotine and sugars might "be varied to win consumer preference for our brands." Griffith pointed out that B&W''s "sales pattern'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'from-1960-1963-has-been', '595', '[from 1960 to 1963] has been positively correlated with the nicotine level of the tobacco in our products." Griffith went on to state that "the nicotine level of B&W cigarettes [studied] was not obtained by accident." He closed by recognizing the marketing department''s role in determining nicotine content in cigarettes, stating, "I think that we can say even now that we can regulate, fairly precisely, the nicotine and sugar levels to almost any desired level management might require. . . ." 102630333-0336 at 0333, 0334, 0335, 0336 (US 23000).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'smith-vice-president-marketing-development', '596', 'Smith, Vice President of Marketing and Development for Lorillard, summarized a meeting on Lorillard''s "5 Year Domestic Cigarette Marketing Plan." Smith wrote:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', '03364986-4988-4987-us-85408-see', '596', '03364986-4988 at 4987 (US 85408); see also 01254896-4898 at 4897 (US 34518); 83250744-0747 at 0746 (US 55650).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'For Decades, Defendants Have Recognized that Controlling Nicotine', 'conap-minnemeyer-explained-objective-lorillard', '597', '(CONAP)." Minnemeyer explained that the objective of Lorillard''s extensive study of the mechanisms by which nicotine delivery could be controlled was a "highly important business objective." 83251036-1044 at 1037 (US 55688).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'specifically-designed-deliver-range-nicotine', '597', 'specifically designed to deliver a range of nicotine doses so that a smoker can obtain her optimal dose from virtually any cigarette on the market, regardless of that cigarette''s nicotine delivery level as measured by the FTC method. Farone WD, 99:10-12; Henningfield WD, 36:8-16.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'farone-tt-10-7-04', '598', 'Farone TT, 10/7/04, 2021:6-13.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'rather-defendants-combine-design-parameters', '599', 'Rather, Defendants combine design parameters to ensure that any particular cigarette delivers a sufficient level of nicotine. Id. at 48:2-6, 84:16-85:6; Henningfield WD, 49:8-53:5, 54:7-15, 55:13- 56:7, 66:14-67:12.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'farone-wd-99-10-12', '599', 'Farone WD, 99:10-12.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'smoking-health-becoming-more-prominent', '600', 'smoking and health was becoming a more prominent subject of public concern, that public interest in less harmful cigarette products could ultimately require reduced levels of nicotine and tar in conventional commercial cigarettes. Harris WD, 139:15-140:10 & Dem. 5. Defendants also recognized that if they addressed smokers'' concerns about the health effects of smoking by reducing the levels of tar in their cigarettes, they might also effect a proportional drop in nicotine. They also recognized that a reduction in nicotine delivery levels which was no longer sufficient to sustain smokers'' addiction could devastate their industry. Defendants therefore set out to design commercial cigarettes that were capable of delivering nicotine across a range of doses that would keep smokers addicted. Henningfield WD, 54:7-15, 55:13-56:7, 66:23-67:12; Farone WD, 72:10-13, 86:18-89:13.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'tobacco-used-manufacture-commercial-cigarettes', '602', 'tobacco used to manufacture commercial cigarettes. It can also be controlled through adding, eliminating, or reducing particular substances from a tobacco blend before it is used as a filler. Farone WD, 37:8-21; 39:11-16; 89:1-23. The tobacco blend is the main component of a cigarette that contributes to nicotine delivery because the blend determines how much nicotine will be in the unsmoked rod. Farone WD, 86:1-7; 107472304-2464 (US 20254); Henningfield WD, 65:22-66:8; Farone WD, 49:6-13; 52:15-23.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'farone-wd-44-2-13-burley', '603', 'Farone WD, 44:2-13. Burley is a strain of tobacco with a higher alkaloid content than Bright, which means that it naturally has more nicotine than Bright tobacco. Burley tobacco is air-cured, which means that it is hung inside a shed to dry where no sunlight will hit it. Id. at 44:14-20, 46:16-20. Oriental tobacco, also referred to as Turkish, is imported and cured through a process called fermentation, meaning that the leaves are packed into moist stacks and fermented. Id. at 44:19-20, 47:8-17.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'nicotine-since-least-1954-document', '605', 'of nicotine since at least 1954. In a document titled "An Outline of Current and Proposed Quality Control, Development and Research for Benson and Hedges," circulated in 1954, Philip Morris explained that comparisons of "analytical data for the blend with estimates of nicotine, tar and pH of the smoke should enable us to set up certain limits or norms within which the chemical composition of the blend must be controlled in order to achieve desired smoking quality." The document recommended:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '1001761472-1484-1474-us-35556', '605', '1001761472-1484, 1474 (US 35556).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '501052852-2856-us-20671', '608', '501052852-2856 (US 20671).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '2-how-develop-flue-cured-burley', '609', '(2) how to develop flue-cured and Burley tobacco with higher nicotine content. 508799518-9519'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '510723285-3286-3286-us-88090', '609', '510723285-3286 at 3286 (US 88090).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '504140118-0127-0118-us-85495', '610', '504140118-0127 at 0118 (US 85495).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'research-june-5-1974-memorandum', '610', 'research. A June 5, 1974 memorandum from R.M. Irby, Jr., Manager of New Products Division, Research and Development, to J.B. McCarthy, Executive Vice President, and copied to J.H. Hager, Executive Vice President, outlined B&W''s research and knowledge on "increasing the nicotine content of reconstituted tobacco." The methods for accomplishing this included: adding nicotine to reconstituted tobacco base sheets, replacing current leaf blends with higher nicotine tobacco, cast- sheeting tobacco "dust" that is high in nicotine content, altering filters, and changing smoke content. Irby discussed studies done to raise the nicotine delivery of Pall Mall and Lucky Strike cigarettes and to raise nicotine delivery in low tar cigarettes. MNAT00533225-3228 (US 85492).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '682515783-5803-us-88089-2072566619-6619-us', '613', '682515783-5803 (US 88089); 2072566619-6619 (US 88087). The Brazilian patent was never disclosed to the FDA.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'its-low-tar-cigarettes-company', '613', 'its low tar cigarettes. Company researchers investigated the effect of increasing the Burley tobacco in its Lucky Strike tobacco blend in 1963 as part of its low tar cigarette studies. The objective of the research "was to determine the effect of increasing the Burley Tobacco in a blend on the yield of nicotine." MNAT00316738-6748 at 6738 (US 21226).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'mnat00367425-7425-us-85490', '615', 'MNAT00367425-7425 (US 85490).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'american-would-bury-its-mistakes', '617', 'American would "bury [its] mistakes," i.e., it would "throw it away and start again with a new blend." Sprinkle PD, Small, 10/16/97, 33:8-34:23.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'camel-newport-shared-two-common', '617', 'Camel, and Newport, shared two common traits: high nicotine content and a high nicotine to tar ratio. With this knowledge, Lorillard blended different tobaccos in an effort to generate a high nicotine to tar ratio blend. By 1973, the company''s one-, three-, and five-year research plans included research to modify tobacco in order to control the delivery of nicotine. 00776195-6201 (US 34293); 526321304-1310 (US 85414); Farone WD, 86:20-87:12; Spears PD, Minnesota v. Philip Morris, 9/23/97, 98:5-100:7.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '00044787-4799-4787-4789-us-34196', '618', '00044787-4799 at 4787, 4789 (US 34196).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'tobacco-filler-continues-present-timothy', '619', 'tobacco filler continues to the present. Timothy Jackson, Chief Operating Officer of Vector Tobacco, a Liggett Group Inc. subsidiary, wrote in an August 27, 2001 e-mail: "We are still considering altering the flue cured mix, without changing the total input ratios, to try and moderately increase nicotine. The hesitancy to do this, however, is the potential resultant increase in nitrosamines." VDOJ25348-5248 (US 64735) (Confidential).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'nicotine-cigarettes-defendants-undertook-extensive', '619', 'nicotine" cigarettes, Defendants undertook extensive efforts to control the ratio of nicotine to tar in order to deliver more nicotine despite the decrease in tar.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'alternatively-reductions-tar-nicotine-ratio', '620', '(or, alternatively, reductions in the tar to nicotine ratio) are, in actuality, referring to increasing the amount of nicotine relative to the amount of tar in cigarette smoke.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'even-if-nicotine-levels-remained', '621', 'even if the nicotine levels remained steady, the nicotine to tar level ratio would actually increase as tar levels decreased. See 1989 Surgeon General''s Report at 85:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'despite-numerous-public-statements-nicotine', '621', 'Despite numerous public statements that "nicotine follows tar," i.e., that the amount of nicotine delivered by a cigarette automatically follows the amount of tar in a fixed ratio, and that smokers would therefore get less nicotine as tar levels dropped, Defendants conducted years of research to develop methods of changing the ratio of nicotine to tar in tobacco smoke.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'products-such-way-allow-smokers', '622', 'products in such a way as to allow smokers to determine the amount of nicotine they inhale and increase the nicotine to tar ratio in that inhaled smoke. Henningfield WD, 43:15-20. As researchers inside the industry explored potentially effective filters for tars, they well understood that if nicotine delivery was affected it could reduce the addictive properties of their product. Industry researchers exploring potentially effective filters for tar understood that affecting nicotine delivery could reduce the addictive properties of their product. Accordingly, cigarette company Defendants took steps to design a filter that would register a lower tar level according to the FTC method but would not reduce nicotine transfer into the body. Their goal was to create a filter that, while lowering tar, would deliver a sufficient dose of nicotine to the lungs in order to sustain a smoker''s addiction. Id. at 43:15-44:13.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'cigarette-smoke-air-during-inhalation', '623', 'cigarette smoke with air during inhalation. Henningfield WD, 46:11-22; Farone WD, 42:11-14. Ventilation holes are created by perforation that can be done with lasers, mechanically, or electrostatically. Henningfield WD, 46:4-7.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'nicotine-tar-ratio-smoke-henningfield', '624', 'nicotine to tar ratio in smoke. Henningfield WD, 63:19-64:4, 64:8-18. The paper used for cigars and hand-rolled cigarettes does not burn well and evenly, and it often self-extinguishes. Cigarette paper used on manufactured cigarettes is different. It is treated with chemicals that can affect nicotine delivery and burn accelerant chemicals that make the cigarettes burn hotter and faster. Some of the chemical additives that affect nicotine delivery in commercial cigarettes are buffering compounds, including alkaline compounds. They make the paper white, keeping the ashes a relatively attractive light grey color, and burn accelerants, such as sodium and potassium citrate. Henningfield WD, 64:5-18.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '62-23-63-3-cigarette-paper', '625', '62:23-63:3. The cigarette paper used by Defendants to manufacture their commercial products is of controlled porosity. Controlling porosity is a means of controlling the composition and lowering the amount of nicotine in smoke measured by the FTC smoking machine by altering the mix of gases, temperature of the burning tobacco, and the speed at which the cigarette is burned. Henningfield WD, 62:22-63:18.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'delivered-smoker-enhance-its-speed', '626', 'delivered to the smoker and enhance its speed of absorption in the body. Alteration of the pH of cigarette smoke was one of the primary areas of research they pursued for this purpose.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '103281081-1112-us-34706-599003691-3695-us', '629', '103281081-1112 (US 34706); 599003691-3695 (US 22077); 103281081-1112 at 1082, 1099 (US'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '1000048537-8552-8539-us-35106-difference', '630', '1000048537-8552 at 8539 (US 35106). This difference is due not to the amount of nicotine but to the form of the nicotine and its availability for absorption in the mouth and lungs. Henningfield WD, 68:2-9; Dixon WD, 13:17-20; 400993160-3331 at 3320 (US 75975*); see also, 500378383- 8386 at 8385 (US 85467).');
INSERT INTO passages (report, section, paragraph_id, page, body) VALUES
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'effects-nicotine-brain-500606138-6153-us', '635', 'effects of nicotine on the brain. 500606138-6153 (US 48334); 509314122-4154 (US 51456); Farone WD, 94:4-7, 97:9-21.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '542001986-1996-1988-1989-us-53135', '637', '542001986-1996 at 1988-1989 (US 53135).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'delivery-example-december-16-1971', '640', 'delivery. For example, a December 16, 1971 report written by D.P. Johnson discusses RJR''s efforts to develop a method "to increase the free nicotine content of the VANTAGE smoke by adding selected salts to the VANTAGE blend." Although Johnson recommended that the costs of adding the salts outweighed the benefits, Reynolds continued its study of various methods for delivering more nicotine to the mainstream cigarette smoke of its products. 504414205-4211 at 4205 (US 50608); Henningfield WD, 78:2-16.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '511223463-3484-3468-us-20840', '641', '511223463-3484 at 3468 (US 20840).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '511223463-3484-3466-us-20840', '641', '511223463-3484 at 3466 (US 20840).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '508381102-1112-1102-1103-1105-us', '645', '508381102-1112 at 1102, 1103, 1105 (US 85469).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'c-brown-williamson-batco', '647', '(c) Brown & Williamson and BATCo'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'nicotine-added-various-salts-extracted', '647', 'Nicotine Added as Various ''Salts'' to Extracted Tobacco Cigarettes," which concluded that'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'report-described-freebase-nicotine-having', '647', 'The report described the freebase nicotine as having a transfer three times greater than that of nicotine citrate, a salt. 400722326-2343 at 2327 (US 21577).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'researcher-came-important-conclusion', '647', 'The researcher came to the important conclusion that'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '100059066-9067-9067-us-20102-2', '647', '100059066-9067 at 9067 (US 20102).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '689201723-1770-1758-1760-us-31049', '648', '689201723-1770 at 1758, 1760 (US 31049).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '65005805-5807-5806-us-85446', '650', '65005805-5807 at 5806 (US 85446).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '510000667-0670-us-51496-emphasis-original', '651', '510000667-0670 (US 51496) (emphasis in original).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '602759-2759-us-53297-see-also', '653', '602759-2759 (US 53297); see also 401020175-0181 (US 47529).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '508104012-4164-4017-us-53249-emphasis', '654', '508104012-4164 at 4017 (US 53249*) (emphasis in original) . The report of this conference also noted that "[a]ll U.S. manufacturers except Liggett use some form of AT [Ammonia Technology] on some cigarette products." Id. at 4016.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'discussing-diammonium-phosphate-dap-additive', '656', 'In discussing diammonium phosphate ("DAP") as an additive, the Handbook states that "[s]ince DAP can only provide ammonia, it can act only as an ameliorant, an impact booster, and satisfaction promoter." 621800840-0899 at 0845 (US 86908).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '30-1980-american-memorandum-from', '657', '30, 1980 American memorandum from N.L. Bodenhamer to Eugene Glock on "Increasing Nicotine Transfer in Smoke" stated:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'test-hypothesis-researchers-conducted-experiment', '657', 'To test this hypothesis, researchers conducted an experiment in which they added 2% or 5% potassium carbonate to American''s Tareyton tobacco blend. Taste tests "suggested that more nicotine had transferred to the smoke, with the 5% being more harsh than the 2%." ATC2570157- 0157 (US 66272); Henningfield WD, 68:23-69:1, 69:21-70:16.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'x003498-3506-3497-us-86972', '658', 'X003498-3506 at 3497 (US 86972).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'sought-improve-smoking-quality-its', '658', 'sought to improve the smoking quality of its reconstituted tobacco. Lorillard believed its research at that time demonstrated that the amount of free nicotine contained in mainstream smoke increased with higher pH, and that higher pH increased impact. Lorillard also studied how the Marlboro reconstituted leaf achieved a higher pH while maintaining a good flavor. Using different additives, types of leaf, and added nicotine, Lorillard tested numerous ways to increase the smoke pH in its reconstituted leaf. 00044833-4841 (US 47324); see also 87644269-4277 (US 56273*).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '00044921-4938-4922-4933-4934-us-34203', '659', '00044921-4938 at 4922, 4933-4934 (US 34203). See also 00778258-8265 (US 34295*); 00121921- 1942 (US 85476); 00778109-8113 (US 22930); 83250763-0765 (US 55655).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '1971-progress-report-project-te-5001', '661', '1971 progress report on project TE-5001 reported that "[i]ncreasing the pH of a medium in which nicotine is delivered increases the physiological effect of the nicotine by increasing the ratio of freebase to acid salt form, the freebase form being more readily transported across physiological membranes." The importance of this finding was explained: "[w]e are pursuing this project with the eventual goal of lowering the total nicotine present in smoke while increasing the physiological effect of the nicotine which is present, so that no physiological effect is lost on nicotine reduction." LG0262125-2126 at 2126 (US 59994).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'ammonia-which-facilitate-nicotine-delivery', '663', 'to ammonia, which facilitate nicotine delivery. Cigarette smoke contains chemicals that can act synergistically to produce effects that might be even more addicting than nicotine alone. Farone WD, 74:4-9. For example, studies by Philip Morris have indicated that levels of acetaldehyde (a chemical involved in alcohol dependence) in smoke can be manipulated through additives so as to produce a mixture of acetaldehyde and nicotine that would be more addictive than either drug alone. DeNoble WD, 31:5-32:15; 1000413881-3964 (US 20100); 1003060443-0503 (US 87091).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', '682633150-3156-3152-us-54206-2', '665', '682633150-3156 at 3152 (US 54206).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Researched, Developed, and Utilized Various Designs and', 'researchers-lorillard-studied-possible-cigarette', '666', 'Researchers for Lorillard studied possible cigarette additives that would facilitate the migration of nicotine from other parts of the cigarette to the cigarette paper, recognizing that "the major portion of the mainstream smoke is generated from the outer periphery of the cigarette." 83896877-6879 at 6878 (US 55922). A 1980 report likewise stated that "[i]t has been demonstrated that the impregnation of cigarette paper with acid can cause migration of nicotine to the periphery. This in turn elevates delivery of nicotine in mainstream smoke." 00114987-4996 at 4987 (US 34264). Lorillard conducted further studies on migration and confirmed these results. 00114962-4975 at 4962 (US 34263); see also 00041734-1735 (US 34189); 000115023-5025 (US 34265); 83896952- 6954 (US 55925).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'control-amount-delivery-nicotine-cigarettes', '666', 'to control the amount and delivery of nicotine in cigarettes, Defendants have denied, repeatedly and publicly, that they manipulate nicotine content and delivery in cigarettes in order to create and sustain addiction. Defendants have also repeatedly and publicly claimed that the levels of nicotine delivered by cigarettes are determined by their levels of tar delivery.17'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'addictiveness-cigarettes-tobacco-industry-s', '667', 'addictiveness of cigarettes and the tobacco industry''s design of cigarettes and manipulation of nicotine. These hearings, before the House of Representatives Subcommittee on Health and the Environment, later became known as the "Waxman Hearings," referring to Subcommittee Chairman Henry Waxman of California. The Chief Executive Officers of six Defendant cigarette manufacturers -- Philip Morris, B&W, RJR, Lorillard, Liggett, and American -- appeared voluntarily at a Subcommittee hearing on April 14, 1994.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'tlt0730001-0850-us-77011', '668', 'TLT0730001-0850 (US 77011).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'philip-morris-asserted-does-nothing', '668', 'Philip Morris asserted that it "does nothing in the processing of tobacco or the manufacture of cigarettes that increases the nicotine in our products above what is naturally found in tobacco."'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'philip-morris-also-stated-ftc', '668', 'Philip Morris also stated that the FTC testing method provided consumers with "information concerning the relative nicotine yields of products that permit them to make an informed choice."'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'tlt0730001-0850-0362-0363-us-77011', '668', 'TLT0730001-0850 at 0362–0363 (US 77011).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'tlt0730001-0850-0546-0556-us-77011', '669', 'TLT0730001-0850 at 0546, 0556 (US 77011) (emphasis in original).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'february-28-1994-letter-mailed', '670', 'In a February 28, 1994 letter mailed to FDA Commissioner Kessler in advance of the Waxman Hearings, Reynolds''s CEO James W. Johnston claimed that "R.J. Reynolds Tobacco Company does not increase the nicotine in its cigarettes above what is found naturally in tobacco." TLT0730001- 0850 at 0368, 0370 (US 77011).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'barclay-cigarette-which-used-tobacco', '672', 'Barclay cigarette, which used tobacco with a nicotine concentration of 2.3% (35% higher than the average amount of nicotine found in tobacco), Riehl did not deny that B&W used a tobacco with a high nicotine concentration to manufacture Barclay cigarettes. Riehl testified, however, that B&W selected the tobacco used for Barclay for its taste. Specifically, Riehl stated: "We blend for taste, not nicotine." TLT073 0001-0850 at 0767 (US 77011).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'tlt0730001-0850-0597-0599-us-77011', '673', 'TLT0730001-0850 at 0597, 0599 (US 77011).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'tlt0730001-0850-0380-us-77011', '674', 'TLT0730001-0850 at 0380 (US 77011).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'ldoj9511212-1230-1220-1221-1222-23-us', '675', 'LDOJ9511212-1230 at 1220, 1221, 1222-23 (US 86933) (Confidential).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', '682637639-7640-7639-us-30999', '676', '682637639-7640 at 7639 (US 30999).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', '2076733633-3633-us-43887', '677', '2076733633-3633 (US 43887).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'bat-does-not-spike-its', '678', '"BAT does not ''spike'' its tobaccos with nicotine. Smoking is not an addiction." 800335882-5886'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', '525722360-2360-us-86935-emphasis-original', '678', '525722360-2360 (US 86935) (emphasis in original).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'timn328214-8215-us-21284', '679', 'TIMN328214-8215 (US 21284).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'tlt0730851-1975-us-77012', '679', 'TLT0730851-1975 (US 77012).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'mtp0030945-0986-0969-us-76202-2078124371-4372', '680', 'MTP0030945-0986 at 0969 (US 76202); 2078124371-4372 at 4371 (US 86938).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'same-corresponding-statements-denying-their', '681', 'the same or corresponding statements denying their ability and efforts to control nicotine.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'defendants-have-over-course-many', '684', 'Defendants have, over the course of many years, time and again -- and with great self-righteousness'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', '---denied-manipulated-nicotine-cigarettes', '684', '-- denied that they manipulated the nicotine in cigarettes so as to increase the addiction and dependence of smokers. Those denials were false.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Made False and Misleading Public Statements', 'different-scientific-approaches-over-last', '685', 'different scientific approaches, over the last forty years, to develop and market less hazardous cigarettes. These Defendants knew that there was an enormous market for such cigarettes and an enormous profit to be made by whichever company was first to achieve success. For that reason, they invested hundreds of millions of dollars, as well as the time and creativity of hundreds of scientists and technical assistants, to reach that goal. They still failed.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Long Acknowledged Internally the Existence of a', 'recognized-consumer-interest-smoking-cigarettes', '686', 'recognized consumer interest in smoking cigarettes that are truly less hazardous. See Harris WD, 140:3-145.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Long Acknowledged Internally the Existence of a', 'brand-manager-mike-mckee-manager', '688', 'Brand Manager, to Mike McKee, a Manager in the New Brands and Strategic Research Department,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Long Acknowledged Internally the Existence of a', 'lloyd-commented-recent-patents-applied', '688', 'Lloyd commented on recent patents applied for or granted to Philip Morris and Imperial Tobacco'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Long Acknowledged Internally the Existence of a', 'company', '688', 'Company:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Have Long Acknowledged Internally the Existence of a', '501541129-1132-1131-us-21740', '688', '501541129-1132 at 1131 (US 21740).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Defendants Received Conflicting Messages From the Government and the Public Health Community About Their Efforts to Create and Market', 'no-bates-jd-043377', '690', '(no bates) (JD 043377).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'smoking-decreasing-levels-all-mainstream', '693', 'smoking by decreasing the levels of all mainstream smoke constituents. Townsend WD, 79:5-9.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'since-1950s-defendant-cigarette-manufacturers', '694', 'Since the 1950s, the Defendant cigarette manufacturers have developed and implemented means for general reduction in commercial cigarettes. Townsend WD, 79:10-16; Whidby WD, 29:4-30:1. Coggins, United States Dep. 6/27/02, 23:2-14.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'develop-potentially-less-hazardous-cigarettes', '697', 'develop potentially less hazardous cigarettes. Townsend WD, 22:6-13. See also id. at 31:1-33:2,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '40-15-41-2-42-20-43', '698', '40:15-41:2, 42:20-43:2; Whidby WD, 29:4-12; 30:17-19. The theory behind selective reduction is that, if a compound in cigarette smoke which is responsible for the inherent health risks of smoking is reduced or eliminated, the health risks of smoking should decrease. Townsend WD, 32:12-21; see also id. at 44:1-6.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'take-place-during-smoking-bap', '698', 'take place during smoking. BaP is only one of the many polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) present in cigarette smoke. Robinson, United States Dep., 11/13/03, 255:8-22.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '6-26-02-227-7-229', '699', '6/26/02, 227:7-229:23; (no bates) JD 060507; Lilly, United States Dep., 5/14/02, 145:5-146:3; Lilly, United States Dep., 5/15/02, 73:24-75:3; Farone TT, 10/6/04, 1693:16-1694:19; 1001532403 (US 35549); 1001532384-2388 at 2385 (US 35548); 1004858161-8166 at 8163 (US 35920); Robinson, United States, Dep., 11/12/03, 209:3-12; 213:7-25; 214:1-10; 255:8-22.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'tumor-promoters-townsend-wd-52', '699', 'tumor promoters. Townsend WD, 52:17-53:2; JD 000742 (at 231).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'ernst-wynder-dietrich-hoffman-published', '701', 'Ernst Wynder and Dietrich Hoffman published an article stating that reduction of phenols in cigarette smoke would be a good path to take to reduce the risks of cigarette smoking. Harris TT, 10/18/04, 2784:14-18. As discussed in more detail below, after the publication of Wynder''s article, Defendants marketed a series of charcoal filtered products that were commercially available throughout the 1960s. Harris TT, 10/18/04, 2784:22-2785:2.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'filter-which-view-philip-morris', '702', 'filter and which was, in the view of a Philip Morris researcher, "superior to anything in the market place" from a health standpoint. Results of research Philip Morris conducted in 1969 on the biological effects of whole fresh smoke and the gas phase of cigarette smoke in unfiltered and charcoal-filtered cigarettes included the finding that, "[c]arbon filters effectively reduce the biologically active components of smoke." 1000036347-6366 at 6354 (US 20070). Philip Morris discontinued production in 1964 after the initial phase of testing, citing poor taste and lack of consumer acceptance. 1000307159-7164 at 7160-61 (US 20092).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'no-bates-us-20092-1-3', '703', '(no bates) (US 20092 at 1-3).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '2075008054-8067-8054-us-87572', '704', '2075008054-8067 at 8054 (US 87572).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'states-dep-6-27-02', '705', 'States Dep., 6/27/02, 122:14-21.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'structures-lining-long-passageways-lungs', '706', 'structures lining the long passageways in the lungs that are responsible for removing foreign matter, including particulate matter. Aldehydes, which are carcinogenic compounds present in cigarette smoke, are one type of ciliastats. Townsend WD, 56:6-59:3; Townsend TT, 3/8/05, 14676:17- 14677:22. Over the years, the cigarette manufacturing Defendants have researched various ways to potentially reduce aldehydes. See, e.g., Townsend WD, 57:4-7; Townsend TT,10/6/04,1747:21-23, 1743:25-1744:3, 1744:17-1747:20; 207458814-8905 at 8822 (JD 054065); Harris TT, 10/18/04,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '2761-8-20-no-bates-jd', '707', '2761:8-20; (no bates) (JD 04426); Robinson, United States Dep., 11/12/03, 211:19-212:11; Spears, BCBS Dep., 3/23/00, 144:4-22; 03645135-5329 at 5303 (JD 020169); 00061704-1752 (JD 020259); 01211241-1245 (JD 024760).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '10-11-01-10-10-11', '708', '10/11/01, 10:10-11:11, 12:15-13:24, 16:22-18:2, 35:6-23, 38:10-39:2, 40:5-21; Robinson, United States Dep., 11/13/03 at 263:17-22, 264:23-265:22, 266:4-267:6.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '1000015245-5246-us-22133-511351011-1019-1017', '709', '1000015245-5246 (US 22133); 511351011-1019 at 1017 (US 88040) (Patent No. 4,516,590, filed November 26, 1982, issued May 14, 1985); see also 2026526349-6353 (US 86964) (Patent No. 4,607,646, submitted in Feb. 1984, issued to Cliff Lilly on August 26, 1986, patenting a method for treating bright tobacco to create a tobacco with Burley''s smoking characteristics, but without Burley''s "less desirable features").'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'precursor-electrically-heated-cigarette-farone', '712', 'precursor to the electrically heated cigarette. Farone TT, 10/7/04 Tr., 1841:21-1842:11. Prior to the Accord, Philip Morris worked on other non-conventional cigarette projects called Delta and Sigma. Lilly United States Dep., 5/15/02, 73:16-17, 78:17-79:4. Project Delta used carbon as the heat source for the tobacco, thereby reducing the temperature under which smoke was generated. Lilly, United States Dep., 5/15/02, 73:16-17, 78:17-79:4. With lower temperature, both combustion and pyrolysis are reduced resulting in reductions in the formation of PAHs and aldehydes, both of which are carcinogenic compounds in cigarette smoke. Farone TT, 10/6/04, 1694:4-16, 1694:20-1695:6.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'cigarette-very-little-nicotine-would', '715', 'that a cigarette with very little nicotine would potentially reduce the harm associated with cigarette smoking, Philip Morris developed and marketed cigarettes with virtually no nicotine. Beran WD, 3-11.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'langenfeld-wd-136-8-30-no', '716', 'Langenfeld WD, 136:8-30; (no bates) (JD 067562 at 2).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'langenfeld-wd-136-8-30-no-2', '716', 'Langenfeld WD, 136:8-30; (no bates) (JD 067562 at 14).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'morris-provide-information-regarding-its', '716', 'Morris provide information regarding its advertising and marketing of Next De-Nic. Ultimately,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'philip-morris-withdrew-other-de-nic', '716', 'Philip Morris withdrew the other "De-Nic" products from the market in 1991, claiming poor consumer acceptance. Thereafter, the FTC closed its investigation of Next De-Nic. Langenfeld WD,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'morris-attempted-identify-synthetic-nicotine', '717', 'Morris attempted to identify a synthetic nicotine analogue that would replace the nicotine naturally found in cigarettes. Development of a synthetic nicotine analogue would reduce the adverse cardiovascular effects of smoking, such as increased blood pressure and increased heart rate. Mele WD, 7:16-8:3; DeNoble WD, 5:7-11, 7:8-22; DeNoble TT, 1/6/05, 8990:10-17; Levy WD, 6:27-31; Levy TT, 2/9/05, 12701:10-25.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '8-16-19-13-11-22-denoble', '718', '8:16-19, 13:11-22; DeNoble TT, 1/6/05, 8993:3-18, 8993:22-8995:4 (DeNoble); Levy TT, 2/9/05,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'research-reduced-retention-smoke-particulate', '718', 'research, reduced the retention of smoke particulate matter in a smoker''s lungs by 63%. Unlike the filters Defendants designed for cigarettes they marketed as "low tar" cigarettes, the "multijet" filter limited one form of smoker compensation -- puffing harder on cigarettes to obtain more nicotine -- and yielded tar and nicotine deliveries to human smokers that closely matched their FTC machine-measured yields. The multijet filter accomplished this by increasing its efficiency as flow rate increased -- that is, the harder a smoker puffed on the cigarette, the less material passed through to the smoker. Farone WD, 133:13-134:1. See 514901941-1966 at 1941 (US 30086); 504210090-0091 (US 29749); see also Townsend TT, 3/8/05, 14790:25-14800:1, 14801:17-14803:9.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'cigarette-heated-rather-than-burned', '720', 'cigarette that heated, rather than burned, tobacco. In the early 1980s, Reynolds''s researchers discovered that a small carbon heat source, combined with an aluminum heat conductor, tobacco and glycerol, could produce smoke without burning the tobacco. Townsend WD, 142:11-21. With that innovation, RJR began work on what would become Premier. The product development objectives for Premier were to simplify the mainstream and sidestream smoke chemistry by eliminating or reducing harmful compounds produced by burning tobacco, to minimize the potential for biological activity (as measured by toxicological assays and tests frequently used to study cigarettes and cigarette smoke), and to minimize environmental tobacco smoke. Townsend WD, 126:6-9; Burger, United States Dep., 7/26/01 71:4-72:8; see also 507141075-1463 (JD 060325). Reynolds succeeded in meeting all of its product development objectives. Townsend WD, 126:10-11; 507141075-1463 at 1080-83 (JD 060325).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'light-brand-primary-factors-limiting', '725', 'Light brand). The primary factors limiting the overall acceptance of Premier in the consumer testing were artificial taste, aftertaste, and aroma. Follow-up research suggested that while taste was the primary contributor to the low overall acceptability of Premier, changes in smoking ritual (e.g., lighting difficulty, absence of visual cues indicating remaining puffs) also played a role. 507543977- 4004 at 3981 (JD 041936).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '000866-457-recent-attempt-market', '726', '000866 (at 457) ("A recent attempt to market a modified cigarette that ''heats rather than burns tobacco'' has not been accepted by consumers."). Premier''s retail share of market in the test cities peaked at 0.57% in the test market''s third week. After that point, Premier began to experience a declining share trend. By February, 1989, Premier''s market share was down to 0.14% and declining. (no bates) (JD 041936 at 50754 3984).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'hhs0741270-1271-jd-065276', '727', 'HHS0741270-1271 (JD 065276).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'hhs0681659-1660-jd-042075', '727', 'HHS0681659-1660 (JD 042075).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'continued-w-ho-does-reynolds', '727', 'It continued, "[w]ho does Reynolds think it is fooling when it uses words like ''controversial compounds'' or ''cleaner.'' These words are nothing but transparent euphemisms for ''healthier'' and'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'safer-hhs0813096-3110-jd-042083-john', '728', '''safer.''" HHS0813096-3110 (JD 042083). John Slade, in his petition, noted that "[p]eople who smoke are not concerned about the dirtiness of smoke; they are concerned about its toxicity. . . .'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'reynolds-fully-aware-course-10', '728', 'Reynolds is fully aware of this, of course." 10/11/88 letter from J. Slade to F.E. Young, FDA,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'hhs0830014-0023-jd-042084', '728', 'HHS0830014-0023 (JD 042084).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'national-institute-drug-abuse-dr', '728', 'National Institute on Drug Abuse, to Dr. Jerome Jaffe, the Director of the HHS Addiction Research'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'center-dr-henningfield-stated', '728', 'Center, Dr. Henningfield stated'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'hhs0130224-0227-0227-jd-065279', '728', 'HHS0130224-0227 at 0227 (JD 065279).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'hhs0880359-0364-0360-us-85828', '730', 'HHS0880359-0364 at 0360 (US 85828).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'director-centers-disease-control-s', '731', 'Director of the Centers for Disease Control''s Office on Smoking and Health, Mr. Hutt commented:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'y-ou-asked-whether-reynolds', '731', '"[Y]ou asked whether Reynolds agreed that cigarettes caused the health problems I had mentioned.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'i-responded-did-not-i', '731', 'I responded that they did not[;]" and "I again responded that Reynolds did not agree that the current cigarette is unsafe and would not contend that the new cigarette is safer or safe." 506147781-7783 at 7782 (US 93089).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'premier-gerald-long-confirmed-rjr', '731', 'Premier, Gerald Long, confirmed RJR''s commitment to protect Defendants'' position that cigarettes are not harmful:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'long-pd-united-states-v', '732', 'Long PD, United States v. Philip Morris, 10/18/01, 86:3-90:25.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '507543977-4004-3986-us-85829-see', '732', '507543977-4004 at 3986 (US 85829); see also deBethizy PD, United States v. Philip Morris, 4/17/02, 122:6-8 (Premier''s "taste was dramatically different and people were unprepared for it").'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'development-another-cigarette-reduced-burning', '733', 'development of another cigarette that reduced the burning temperature to reduce the formation and delivery of harmful constituents. Reynolds first test-marketed this product -- Eclipse -- seven years later, in 1996. Unlike Premier, Reynolds began marketing Eclipse in April 2000 with a claim that it may present less risk of lung cancer, chronic bronchitis, and emphysema. Townsend WD, 143:13- 144:3, 148:7-11, 152:5-8; (no bates) (US 85276); (no bates) (US 85277) (indicating that Reynolds R&D staff were working to achieve major improvements in Eclipse''s lightability and taste/satisfaction).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'acknowledging-reynolds-published-many-studies', '738', '(acknowledging that Reynolds published many studies in peer reviewed journals). Moreover,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'reynolds-s-patents-relating-eclipse', '738', 'Reynolds''s patents relating to Eclipse are publicly available. See Richter United States Dep. 3/8/02,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '207-23-208-12', '738', '207:23-208:12.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '109-17-110-7-id-222', '738', '109:17-110:7; id. at 222:11-21.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'experience-scientific-recognition-fields-analytical', '739', '"experience and scientific recognition in the fields of analytical chemistry, genetic toxicology, pharmacology, inhalation toxicology, carcinogenesis, veterinary and human pathology, clinical pulmonary diseases, and clinical cardiology." (no bates) (JD 060235 at 1).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'no-bates-jd-060235-3', '739', '(no bates) (JD 060235 at 3); Townsend WD, 151:13-22.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'no-bates-jd-060235-32', '739', '(no bates) (JD 060235 at 32, 34); see also Townsend WD, 151:1-12.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'reynolds-contacted-fda-early-2000', '739', 'Reynolds contacted the FDA in early 2000 and requested a meeting to discuss the company''s plans to test market Eclipse. 520737199-7200 (JD 041953). In March 2000, Reynolds briefed the FDA on Eclipse and provided the FDA with a summary of the science behind Eclipse. 521556628-6628 (JD 041956); 521561627-1627 (JD 041958); see also 521561595-1595 (JD 041957) (advising of test market in Dallas/Fort Worth area and providing letter and large brochure that will be sent to physicians and health care workers). Reynolds''s scientists also presented data on Eclipse to the Society of Toxicology, the International Congress of Toxicology and the Tobacco Chemists Conference. Richter United States Dep. 3/8/02, 206:3-15; 206:22-208:20.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '517156964-6984-6973-jd-041949', '741', '517156964-6984 at 6973 (JD 041949).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'create-tobacco-burning-cigarette-had', '742', 'was to create a "tobacco burning" cigarette that had the potential to reduce health risks. Gentry WD, 4:4-17, 5:3-6; Gentry TT, 10/14/04, 2385:22-2386:03; id. at 2388:7-8; see also Gentry TT, 3/8/05, 14853:15-14854:13.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'compared-marlboro-light-85s-reporting', '745', '(compared to Marlboro Light 85s, reporting substantial reductions in nitrosamines, which are possible human carcinogens, and other oxides of nitrogen, which may be linked to emphysema), 28:21-29:3; (2) reductions in biological activity in both in vitro and in vivo tests, Gentry WD, 29:4- 34:1 (discussing 517400643-0671 (US 30327) (Confidential), showing that EW prototypes had "statistically lower" mutagenicity scores compared to Marlboro Light 85s), 34:2-42:19 (discussing genotoxicity results reported in 520984125-4133 (US 80287), 521967676-7677 (US 30519)), 46:18- 56:25 (discussing cytotoxicity results reported in 520984104-4117 (US 80285), 510941930-1938 (US 89101), and 510959750-9752 at 9750, 9752 (US 51536)), 42:23-44:13 and 44:17-46:17 (discussing in vivo sensory/Alarie irritation test results reported in 510768455 (US 87557) and 511325258-5260 (US 30011)); (3) success in human smoker studies, Gentry WD, 58:10-61:10 (discussing 520009013-9027 (US 89100)); and (4) review of this research by a panel of "independent" scientists, Gentry WD, 62:13-68:12 (discussing the data presented to the panel in 515305298-5537 (US 89102) and the panel''s report, 518379726-9740 (US 30345)).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '518793615-3629-jd-067729-fall-off-winston', '747', '518793615-3629 (JD 067729). The fall-off in Winston Select/EW market share in the second year of introduction was much more dramatic than in non-EW test markets. 12/18/96 Memorandum from R.C. Pasterczyk to E.C. Leary and J.D. Weber, 517005979-5980 (JD 067723).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'reynolds-200-page-report-containing-legal', '748', 'Reynolds with a 200-page report containing legal analysis and recommendations. Juchatz TT, 11/22/04, 6687:16-6688:25 (discussing 515873569-3776 at 3575 (US 85886)).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'townsend-admitted-urged-taking-aggressive', '749', '(Townsend admitted that he urged taking an aggressive approach to marketing EW with health claims).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '70028426-8429-8428-8429-us-22184-townsend', '750', '70028426-8429 at 8428-8429 (US 22184); Townsend TT, 3/8/05, 14715:6-15, 14716:7-14718:25, 14722:13-14723:1.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'ill-effects-smoking-included-harm', '751', 'ill effects of smoking" that included harm from constituents in the gas phase of cigarette smoke, including carbon monoxide ("artherosclerosis, permeable arteries, displacement of oxygen in blood"), nitrogen oxide ("obstructive pulmonary disease, emphysema"), and hydrogen cyanide ("cilatoxic"). In response, B&W developed FACT, a "low gas" cigarette to compete with low tar products:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '777076768-6792-6773-6790-us-54623', '751', '777076768-6792 at 6773, 6790 (US 54623).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '66705296-9299-9296-us-69068-777076771-6792', '752', '66705296-9299 at 9296 (US 69068); 777076771-6792 at 6771 (US 54623) (four prior attempts by other manufacturers to market low gas products had failed to achieve significant penetration because of "an inability of the positioning to communicate … distinct health hazards arising from cigarette gases."). A B&W Brand Manager stated explicitly in a memorandum to, among others, the Vice President of Brand Management:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '667059296-9299-9296-us-69068-emphasis', '752', '667059296-9299 at 9296 (US 69068) (emphasis in original).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'reduce-delivery-harmful-compounds-smoke', '752', 'reduce delivery of harmful compounds in smoke. Ariel was a ceramic tube placed in the middle of a conventional cigarette to run the complete length of the rod. The tube was connected to a mouthpiece, which was isolated from the tobacco that surrounded the ceramic rod. Nicotine or nicotine plus flavor was placed inside the tube so that when the tobacco burned, the nicotine and flavor were released when the hot zone reached that portion of the ceramic tube. In this way, the tobacco never underwent combustion. Farone WD, 177:22-178:12. It was patented in 1964 and was based on research done for BATCo at Battelle Laboratories. (no bates) (US 20581 at 301121935). Ariel had the look and feel of a regular cigarette. Farone WD, 177:10-179:7.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '301121935-1936-us-20581-see-also', '753', '301121935-1936 (US 20581); see also 301121911-1917 at 1917 (US 22023) (February 18, 1963, Battelle report confirming that "it was possible to smoke a complete cigarette and get some satisfaction out of it. These experiments make it appear very likely that a satisfactory device can be developed.")'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'id-4279', '754', 'Id. at 4279.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '3-project-airbus', '755', '(3) Project Airbus'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'c-poor-delivery-efficiency-aerosols', '755', 'C Poor delivery efficiency or aerosols/flavors.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'c-messy-debris-after-burning', '755', 'C Messy debris after burning.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'c-fallout-aerosol-generator-during', '755', 'C Fallout of aerosol generator during smoking.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'id-620611370', '756', 'Id. at 620611370.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'conventional-potentially-reduced-exposure-product-advance', '756', 'conventional potentially reduced-exposure product. Advance contains tobacco cured using a patented process to reduce levels of TSNAs – tobacco-specific nitrosamines that are normally formed during the curing process and have been identified as potential carcinogens – and utilizes a new patented triple filter to further reduce deliveries of toxins produced during combustion. Scientists at B&W believed Advance was a potentially reduced-exposure cigarette. Wessel 30(b)(6) PD, United States v. Philip Morris, 3/19/03, 42:1-43:1 (Confidential); Harris WD, 231:4-10; Honeycutt PD, United States v. Philip Morris, 4/23/02, 13:23-15:6, 61:3-66:6; USX5110274-0292 (US 89063); StarUSvPM000251-0363 (US 85920) (Confidential).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'wessel-30-b-6-united', '757', 'Wessel 30(b)(6), United States Dep., 3/19/03, at 71:16-72:17.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', '271098483-8484-8483-je-036407', '759', '271098483-8484 at 8483 (JE 036407).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'c-both-state-there-no', '759', 'C Both state there is no such thing as a safe cigarette.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'compare-star-s-onserts-no', '759', 'Compare Star''s onserts (no bates) (US 52963) with (no bates) (US 88038)) and Brown &'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'williamson-s-onsert-no-bates', '759', 'Williamson''s onsert (no bates) (US 87216). Both onserts contained the mandatory Surgeon'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'general-s-warnings-see-id', '759', 'General''s warnings. See id. Both Brown & Williamson''s and Star Tobacco''s onserts contain similar statements about compensation and the limitations of the FTC method for measuring tar and nicotine yields. Id.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'compare-star-s-onserts-no-2', '760', 'Compare Star''s onserts (no bates) (US 52963) with (no bates) (US 88038) and Brown &'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'As Part of the Effort to Make Less Hazardous Cigarettes, Defendants', 'williamson-s-onsert-no-bates-2', '760', 'Williamson''s onsert (no bates) (US 87216.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'TOBACCO PRODUCTS ARE ADDICTIVE AND POSE', 'serious-health-hazards', '760', 'SERIOUS HEALTH HAZARDS"; and'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'TOBACCO PRODUCTS ARE ADDICTIVE AND POSE', 'compare-524942388-2389-no-bates-us', '761', 'Compare 524942388-2389 (no bates) (US 52963) with 524942390-2391 (US 88038*) with TLT0960001-0002 (US 87216) (onsert to B&W Advance). See also Wessel 30(b)(6) PD, United States v. Philip Morris, 19:3-22:23; Blackie TT, 10/26/04, 3897:15-3900:15.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'TOBACCO PRODUCTS ARE ADDICTIVE AND POSE', 'mellen-united-states-dep-7', '761', 'Mellen, United States Dep., 7/22/04, at 314:19-315:23. Neither NAAG, nor its individual member Attorney Generals, have complained about Brown & Williamson''s onsert for, or marketing of, Advance. Id.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'TOBACCO PRODUCTS ARE ADDICTIVE AND POSE', 'structures-lining-lung-passageways-are', '761', 'structures lining the lung passageways that are responsible for removing foreign matter such as particulate matter from cigarette smoke, become immobilized, or static, and cease their cleansing function. Lorillard conducted a significant amount of research relating to one compound in particular, phenol methyl oxadiazole ("PMO"), as a possible solution to the problem of ciliastasis.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'TOBACCO PRODUCTS ARE ADDICTIVE AND POSE', 'see-e-g-no-bates', '762', 'See, e.g., (no bates) (JD 020259) (reporting a range of 1969-1970 PMO biological studies);'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'TOBACCO PRODUCTS ARE ADDICTIVE AND POSE', 'internal-research-led-conclude-less', '762', 'that internal research led it to conclude was less hazardous to smokers. Liggett saw XA as a way to increase its market share, which had fallen to around 5%, by attracting smokers who desired a cigarette that was potentially less harmful than the cigarette they had been smoking. Ross PD, Washington v. American Tobacco, 10/22/98, 97:1-98:25; Harris WD, 174:13-175:2.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'TOBACCO PRODUCTS ARE ADDICTIVE AND POSE', 'pd-cipollone-v-liggett-11', '764', 'PD, Cipollone v. Liggett, 11/26/85, 191:17-193:18. Ernest Pepples, B&W''s representative on the Tobacco Institute''s Committee of Counsel, threatened Liggett''s "very existence" if it marketed the XA cigarette, including freezing Liggett out of joint defense agreements, and perhaps excluding Liggett from the Tobacco Institute as well, because selling XA would be an admission against the interest of all Cigarette Company Defendants. Meyer PT, Washington v. American Tobacco, 11/10/98, 5511:4-5518:13.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'The Government Has Not Proven by a Preponderance of the Evidence', 'ny-company-discovering-innovation-permitting', '764', 'that "[a]ny company discovering an innovation permitting the fabrication of an essentially safe cigarette would share the discovery with others in the industry and no domestic company would use intact animals [in] in-house bio-medical research." TT, 9/21/04, 122:3-7 ("Gov''t Opening Stmt.). The Government claims that Defendants did this, "[f]irst, to avoid generating evidence that a potentially hazardous cigarette was necessary or possible; and second, to avoid generating internal evidence showing that some of their current products were likely less harmful than others." Id. at'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'The Government Has Not Proven by a Preponderance of the Evidence', 'harris-wd-124-1-12', '766', 'Harris WD, 124:1-12.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'The Government Has Not Proven by a Preponderance of the Evidence', '0001607055-7061-7058-us-76155-single', '767', '0001607055-7061 at 7058 (US 76155). This single sentence reference is not sufficient to prove, by a preponderance of the evidence, that such an "Agreement" existed.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'The Government Has Not Proven by a Preponderance of the Evidence', 'dr-harris-acknowledges-memo-indicates', '768', 'Dr. Harris acknowledges that the memo indicates that (1) "[t]hroughout the domestic industry, the ''Gentlemen''s agreements were operative in the early days," (2) one-half of this "early days" agreement involved sharing discoveries that would permit the manufacturing of a "safe" cigarette, and that (3) at least two companies violated the alleged agreement not to conduct in-house biological research. 501543470-3517 at 3504 (US 21737). Dr. Harris also concedes that this document suggests that the alleged gentleman''s agreement became inoperative. Harris WD, 160:3-6. Most significantly, Dr. Farone conceded that, despite this alleged agreement, all companies were, in fact, doing in-house biological testing "at one point or another" and that at least Reynolds and Lorillard were doing in-house testing on live animals. Farone TT, 10/12/04, 2060:25-2061:21.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'The Government Has Not Proven by a Preponderance of the Evidence', 'court-concludes-government-has-failed', '768', 'the Court concludes that the Government has failed to carry its burden of proving, by a preponderance of the evidence, that Defendants deliberately chose to sabotage the successful marketing and production of less hazardous cigarettes. As these Facts demonstrate, Philip Morris and RJR, in particular, as well as Lorillard and Liggett to a lesser extent, spent many years, enormous amounts of money, and the creative energies of their top scientists to investigate different approaches to production of cigarettes which would present fewer health risks to the public.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'The Government Has Not Proven by a Preponderance of the Evidence', 'being-less-harmful-than-conventional', '770', 'as being less harmful than conventional cigarettes. That claim is false, as these Findings of Fact demonstrate. By making these false claims, Defendants have given smokers an acceptable alternative to quitting smoking, as well as an excuse for not quitting.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Section V: (B)(3), supra (addiction)). Defendants'' internal documents demonstrate their understanding that, in order to obtain an amount of nicotine sufficient to satisfy their addiction, smokers of low tar cigarettes modify their smoking behavior, or "compensate," for the reduced nicotine yields by taking more frequent puffs, inhaling smoke more deeply, holding smoke in their lungs longer, covering cigarette ventilation holes with fingers or lips, and/or smoking more cigarettes. See Section V(E)(2)(b), infra (smoker compensation). As a result of this nicotine-driven smoker behavior, smokers of light cigarettes boost their intake of tar, thus negating what Defendants have long promoted as the primary health-related benefit of light cigarettes: lower tar intake. 2027. Defendants did not disclose the full extent and depth of their knowledge and understanding of smoker compensation to the public health community or to government regulators. 2028. Defendants'' conduct relating to low tar cigarettes was intended to further their overarching economic goal: to keep smokers smoking; to stop smokers from quitting; to encourage people, especially young people, to start smoking; and to maintain or increase corporate profits.', 'history-health-claims', '772', 'a. History of Health Claims'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Section V: (B)(3), supra (addiction)). Defendants'' internal documents demonstrate their understanding that, in order to obtain an amount of nicotine sufficient to satisfy their addiction, smokers of low tar cigarettes modify their smoking behavior, or "compensate," for the reduced nicotine yields by taking more frequent puffs, inhaling smoke more deeply, holding smoke in their lungs longer, covering cigarette ventilation holes with fingers or lips, and/or smoking more cigarettes. See Section V(E)(2)(b), infra (smoker compensation). As a result of this nicotine-driven smoker behavior, smokers of light cigarettes boost their intake of tar, thus negating what Defendants have long promoted as the primary health-related benefit of light cigarettes: lower tar intake. 2027. Defendants did not disclose the full extent and depth of their knowledge and understanding of smoker compensation to the public health community or to government regulators. 2028. Defendants'' conduct relating to low tar cigarettes was intended to further their overarching economic goal: to keep smokers smoking; to stop smokers from quitting; to encourage people, especially young people, to start smoking; and to maintain or increase corporate profits.', 'prosecuted-defendant-cigarette-manufacturers-some', '773', 'prosecuted the Defendant cigarette manufacturers'' for some of these health claims. See In the Matter of American Tobacco Co., 47 F.T.C. 1393 (F.T.C. 1951); R.J. Reynolds Tobacco Co., 46 F.T.C. 706 (1950), modified, 192 F.2d 535 (7th Cir. 1951), on remand, 48 F.T.C. 682 (1952); Federal Trade Commission v. Liggett & Myers Tobacco Co., 108 F. Supp. 573 (S.D.N.Y. 1952), affirmed on opinion below, 203 F.2d 955 (2d Cir. 1953); P. Lorillard Co. v. Federal Trade Commission, 186 F.2d 52, 56-57 (4th Cir. 1950).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Section V: (B)(3), supra (addiction)). Defendants'' internal documents demonstrate their understanding that, in order to obtain an amount of nicotine sufficient to satisfy their addiction, smokers of low tar cigarettes modify their smoking behavior, or "compensate," for the reduced nicotine yields by taking more frequent puffs, inhaling smoke more deeply, holding smoke in their lungs longer, covering cigarette ventilation holes with fingers or lips, and/or smoking more cigarettes. See Section V(E)(2)(b), infra (smoker compensation). As a result of this nicotine-driven smoker behavior, smokers of light cigarettes boost their intake of tar, thus negating what Defendants have long promoted as the primary health-related benefit of light cigarettes: lower tar intake. 2027. Defendants did not disclose the full extent and depth of their knowledge and understanding of smoker compensation to the public health community or to government regulators. 2028. Defendants'' conduct relating to low tar cigarettes was intended to further their overarching economic goal: to keep smokers smoking; to stop smokers from quitting; to encourage people, especially young people, to start smoking; and to maintain or increase corporate profits.', 'december-17-1959-ftc-informed', '776', 'On December 17, 1959, the FTC informed tobacco manufacturers that it henceforth would bar all health claims in advertising, including "all representations of low or reduced tar or nicotine, whether by filtration or otherwise." 1005150070 (JD 004534). The FTC further "inform[ed] the industry that in its opinion the evidence then available would support a complaint against any marketer who made any reference to tar or nicotine content, charging that such a reference was false and misleading." 670310575-588 (JD 040931 at 3).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Section V: (B)(3), supra (addiction)). Defendants'' internal documents demonstrate their understanding that, in order to obtain an amount of nicotine sufficient to satisfy their addiction, smokers of low tar cigarettes modify their smoking behavior, or "compensate," for the reduced nicotine yields by taking more frequent puffs, inhaling smoke more deeply, holding smoke in their lungs longer, covering cigarette ventilation holes with fingers or lips, and/or smoking more cigarettes. See Section V(E)(2)(b), infra (smoker compensation). As a result of this nicotine-driven smoker behavior, smokers of light cigarettes boost their intake of tar, thus negating what Defendants have long promoted as the primary health-related benefit of light cigarettes: lower tar intake. 2027. Defendants did not disclose the full extent and depth of their knowledge and understanding of smoker compensation to the public health community or to government regulators. 2028. Defendants'' conduct relating to low tar cigarettes was intended to further their overarching economic goal: to keep smokers smoking; to stop smokers from quitting; to encourage people, especially young people, to start smoking; and to maintain or increase corporate profits.', 'foundation-support-rule-requiring-positive', '776', 'foundation to support a Rule requiring a positive statement [of average ''tar'' and nicotine yields] in [cigarette] labeling and advertising." Letter of 4/11/66 from Paul Rand Dixon, FTC Chairman, to Sen. Warren Magnuson (670310575-588), JD 040931 (at 3).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Section V: (B)(3), supra (addiction)). Defendants'' internal documents demonstrate their understanding that, in order to obtain an amount of nicotine sufficient to satisfy their addiction, smokers of low tar cigarettes modify their smoking behavior, or "compensate," for the reduced nicotine yields by taking more frequent puffs, inhaling smoke more deeply, holding smoke in their lungs longer, covering cigarette ventilation holes with fingers or lips, and/or smoking more cigarettes. See Section V(E)(2)(b), infra (smoker compensation). As a result of this nicotine-driven smoker behavior, smokers of light cigarettes boost their intake of tar, thus negating what Defendants have long promoted as the primary health-related benefit of light cigarettes: lower tar intake. 2027. Defendants did not disclose the full extent and depth of their knowledge and understanding of smoker compensation to the public health community or to government regulators. 2028. Defendants'' conduct relating to low tar cigarettes was intended to further their overarching economic goal: to keep smokers smoking; to stop smokers from quitting; to encourage people, especially young people, to start smoking; and to maintain or increase corporate profits.', 'decision-allow-advertising-tar-nicotine', '780', 'decision to allow advertising of tar and nicotine yields so long as the Cambridge Filter Method was used, the FTC invited cigarette manufacturers to comment in detail on the precise method to be used to measure tar and nicotine yields. See, e.g., (no bates) (JD 040780); (no bates) (JD 003620).25');
INSERT INTO passages (report, section, paragraph_id, page, body) VALUES
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Section V: (B)(3), supra (addiction)). Defendants'' internal documents demonstrate their understanding that, in order to obtain an amount of nicotine sufficient to satisfy their addiction, smokers of low tar cigarettes modify their smoking behavior, or "compensate," for the reduced nicotine yields by taking more frequent puffs, inhaling smoke more deeply, holding smoke in their lungs longer, covering cigarette ventilation holes with fingers or lips, and/or smoking more cigarettes. See Section V(E)(2)(b), infra (smoker compensation). As a result of this nicotine-driven smoker behavior, smokers of light cigarettes boost their intake of tar, thus negating what Defendants have long promoted as the primary health-related benefit of light cigarettes: lower tar intake. 2027. Defendants did not disclose the full extent and depth of their knowledge and understanding of smoker compensation to the public health community or to government regulators. 2028. Defendants'' conduct relating to low tar cigarettes was intended to further their overarching economic goal: to keep smokers smoking; to stop smokers from quitting; to encourage people, especially young people, to start smoking; and to maintain or increase corporate profits.', 'no-bates-jd-004362-2', '781', '(no bates) (JD 004362 at 2). See also United States'' Obj. and Answers to Joint Defs'' Modified'),
