('us-v-philip-morris', 'Despite Their Internal Knowledge, Defendants'' Marketing and Public', '1972-during-time-when-philip', '917', '1972, during the time when Philip Morris introduced Marlboro Lights, its first "light" cigarette, explained the intended meaning of the "lights" descriptor. Morgan stated that, from the very beginning, the "lights" descriptor was intended to communicate that the brand was low in tar -- as opposed to a brand that was lighter in taste:'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Despite Their Internal Knowledge, Defendants'' Marketing and Public', 'morgan-pd-philip-morris-inc-2', '918', 'Morgan PD, Philip Morris Inc. v. R.J. Reynolds Industries, Inc., 10/15/74, 4:9-10; 10:15-11:4;'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Despite Their Internal Knowledge, Defendants'' Marketing and Public', '78-20-79-14-79-25-80', '918', '78:20-79:14; 79:25-80:5; 81:8-12; 85:23-86:4; Morgan PD, Philip Morris Inc., 11/25/74, 247:11-14.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Despite Their Internal Knowledge, Defendants'' Marketing and Public', 'lower-tar-nicotine-even-though', '918', '"lower tar and nicotine" even though its own marketing research indicated that consumers interpreted that phrase as meaning that the cigarettes not only contained comparatively less tar and nicotine, but also that they were a healthier option. Morgan PD, Price v. Philip Morris, Inc., 6/15/02,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Despite Their Internal Knowledge, Defendants'' Marketing and Public', '45-2-45-25-45-2-46', '918', '45:2-45:25, 45:2-46:25, 47:2-47:25, 48:2-48:25, 49:2-49:25, 50:2-50:25, 51:2-51:5, 52:15-52:20.'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Despite Their Internal Knowledge, Defendants'' Marketing and Public', 'part-image-philip-morris-marketing', '919', 'Part of the image that Philip Morris was marketing was the concept of lowered tar and nicotine.'),
