('us-v-philip-morris', 'PEOPLE WHO SMOKE FILTER CIGARETTES . . . ARE MORE CONSCIOUSLY IN CONFLICT ABOUT SMOKING . . . . THEY CAN''T COMPLETELY ENJOY SMOKING BECAUSE THEY KNOW IT IS NOT HEALTHY. . . . THEY MAY BE RECEPTIVE TO ADVERTISING WHICH HELPS THEM ESCAPE FROM', 'carlton-discussed-marketing-strategies-these', '984', 'Carlton," discussed marketing strategies for these three brands. In the "Advertising Strategy'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'PEOPLE WHO SMOKE FILTER CIGARETTES . . . ARE MORE CONSCIOUSLY IN CONFLICT ABOUT SMOKING . . . . THEY CAN''T COMPLETELY ENJOY SMOKING BECAUSE THEY KNOW IT IS NOT HEALTHY. . . . THEY MAY BE RECEPTIVE TO ADVERTISING WHICH HELPS THEM ESCAPE FROM', 'statement-carlton-report-noted-focus', '984', 'Statement" for Carlton, the report noted that in focus group interviews "the ''health'' problem is most frequently mentioned, but people tend to ignore the negatives and continue to smoke out of pleasure or habit." The report went on to say that "Carlton''s copy strategy for 1973/1974 will continue to be straight forward and factual, appealing to those smokers whose concern for ''health'' hazards leads them to seek out a cigarette with truly low ''tar'' and nicotine content." ATC2472182-2243 at 2216,'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'PEOPLE WHO SMOKE FILTER CIGARETTES . . . ARE MORE CONSCIOUSLY IN CONFLICT ABOUT SMOKING . . . . THEY CAN''T COMPLETELY ENJOY SMOKING BECAUSE THEY KNOW IT IS NOT HEALTHY. . . . THEY MAY BE RECEPTIVE TO ADVERTISING WHICH HELPS THEM ESCAPE FROM', 'advertisements-attached-letter-stated-compare', '986', 'The advertisements attached to the letter stated: "Compare to your brand. . . . Box King -- lowest of all brands -- less than 0.01 mg. tar, 0.002 mg. nic. Carlton is lowest. . . . U.S. Gov''t Report -- no brand lower than Carlton Box King -- less than 0.5 mg. tar, 0.05 mg. nic. . . . FTC Report Mar. ''83." 991034809-4816 at 4809, 4816 (US 85113); see also (US 7536) (1983 Carlton advertisement that appeared in Sports Illustrated magazine).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'PEOPLE WHO SMOKE FILTER CIGARETTES . . . ARE MORE CONSCIOUSLY IN CONFLICT ABOUT SMOKING . . . . THEY CAN''T COMPLETELY ENJOY SMOKING BECAUSE THEY KNOW IT IS NOT HEALTHY. . . . THEY MAY BE RECEPTIVE TO ADVERTISING WHICH HELPS THEM ESCAPE FROM', 'another-carlton-advertisement-campaign-from', '986', 'Another Carlton advertisement campaign from the late 1980s also had lowest tar as its centerpiece and implied a United States Government endorsement, listing Carlton as having lower tar than Philip Morris''s Merit and RJR''s Vantage cigarettes:'),
