('us-v-philip-morris', 'Despite the Overwhelming Evidence to the Contrary, Defendants'' Public', 'us-21271-timn0015615-5617-us-21265', '1205', '(US 21271); TIMN0015615-5617 (US 21265); 2022976326-6335 (US 20370); ATX0402940563190. For example, on April 27, 1964, Philip Morris, B&W, Lorillard, Liggett, RJR, and American Tobacco, through the Tobacco Institute, issued a press release, titled "Cigarette Manufacturers Announce Advertising Code," to announce their adoption of the Cigarette Advertising Code establishing "uniform standards for cigarette advertising." 2065081133-1135 at 1133 (US 20517).'),
('us-v-philip-morris', 'Despite the Overwhelming Evidence to the Contrary, Defendants'' Public', '2070557699-7702-us-20519-ftc-report', '1206', '2070557699-7702 (US 20519). The FTC Report criticized cigarette advertising for exploiting these loopholes by appearing during television shows with audiences where at least 45% of the viewers were under twenty-one; for portraying physical activity as long as the smoker is not a participant; and for implying that smoking contributes to success, even if it is not essential to it. 85872480-2503 at 24-27 (US 22148).'),
